const CHANGES=[
  ['2026.08.23.0',[
    'Fixed: in the Neon theme, the Hard button was the only one of the four rating buttons that did not glow. Neon arrived when there were three rating buttons; Hard became a permanent fourth three days later, and neither of the two lists that give those buttons their glow was revisited. Both now name all four, and the app\'s own test suite checks every one of them so a fifth grade could not slip through the same gap.',
    'Fixed: the theme picker previews each theme\'s rating colours as small dots underneath its name, and there were only three of them -- Again, Good and Easy. Hard is there now, in the buttons\' own order, so a theme\'s Hard colour can be judged before switching to it rather than after.',
    'New: press S to end a session. It goes back to the front screen exactly as tapping the back arrow does -- the clock stops, the sitting closes, and anything not yet synced is sent on its way. From the front screen the same S starts studying again, which is the first keyboard shortcut that screen has ever had. The key is printed on the back arrow when hints are switched on. Escape is deliberately not used: the browser keeps that one for leaving full screen and will not give it up, and it is also what cancels this app\'s confirmation prompts, where it has always meant "no". S is the key the most widely used flashcard program uses for this, chosen for the same reason its bury and set-aside keys were adopted here.',
    'New: "What is in here", a short guided walk found in Settings beside the manual. It is written for somebody looking at the work rather than somebody learning the words: each claim comes with a control that runs the real scheduler, so it can be checked rather than taken. It says plainly which parts are borrowed -- the scheduling algorithm, and measuring your true retention, which the most widely used flashcard program has done since its version 24.11 -- before claiming anything. Nothing on that screen can reach your progress: it works on a card of its own and never saves.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.21.2',[
    'New: a fourth study card shape, Column -- the word, a divider, then the answer underneath it, with both left on screen. This is exactly what a phone has always done; what is new is being able to ask for it on a wide screen, where the two-pane card was previously the only stacked-free option. It is also, to the element, how the most widely used flashcard program arranges its own default card. The setting still only affects a screen wide enough for the two-pane layout -- a phone stacks either way -- and the help text under it now says so properly, naming what the phone actually does rather than only that the setting does not apply to it.',
    'Housekeeping: the app now records, once a day, how many words stand in each stage -- still to meet, in short-term steps, settling, holding, set aside. Nothing shows it yet. It is being collected because it is the one thing about your progress that cannot be worked out afterwards: the review log stores the interval each rating set, not the strength behind it, so a chart of how your words matured over time can be rebuilt from it only roughly -- on a real check it put a third of the holding pile in the wrong band. Two similar figures, your daily pass rate and your daily lapses, have been recorded since the first day and need no such help. The sample costs about fifty bytes a day.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.21.1',[
    'New: the set-aside pile is now drilled in groups. Until now a focus session was a rotating slice -- 15 words picked from whichever had not been drilled lately -- so any one word came round only every eight sessions or so, and the three-separate-good-days rule could take dozens of sessions to fire. Now the pile sits in one fixed order and is cut into groups of about twenty (the size is yours, 10 to 25, and the sizes flex so the pile splits evenly rather than leaving a three-word group at the end). The same words come up every time a group is drilled, until each earns its way out. Drill one group on a tired day or all seven on a keen one, as many times as you like -- only the first look each day counts toward a word\'s three days, exactly as before. The order is a fixed shuffle, not the order the words were learnt in: set-aside dates follow the order words were introduced, so "oldest first" would have rebuilt the very cohorts that failed together the first time; a mixed group spreads the many-meaning words among easier ones. It is the same order on every device, with nothing to sync.',
    'New: a word that earns its three days leaves its group for a tray at the top of the drill screen, and waits there until you release it -- one button brings the whole tray back to normal review, whenever you choose. Nothing leaves the pile on its own, and nothing joins your study queue without your tap. A word set aside while still learning comes back as new, as before; one set aside from real review keeps its history.',
    'New: a word that has had ten separate drill days without earning out is deferred the next time it is missed -- its count restarts and it goes to the back of the order, into a group of its own marked Deferred. Still set aside, still drillable whenever you choose; only its place changes, so the front groups keep flowing instead of slowly filling with the words that never leave. A word mid-streak on its tenth day is left alone -- it is about to earn out.',
    'New: the line under a drill card now reports what today\'s first look did -- "day 1 of 3 banked", "missed -- the count starts again from zero", or that the word has just been deferred -- not only the moment it earns out. The chooser shows each group\'s size, how many of its words are on day one or day two, and whether it has been drilled today; the home screen\'s pile line adds how many groups there are, once the pile splits into more than one.',
    'New: after you miss a word in the drill, its note -- a mnemonic, the word it gets confused with -- appears under the answer, with a button to add or edit one; in the look-alike matching round, each word you mispaired gets its own. Never before you have answered, so the round stays a real test of recall.',
    'Changed: the gap a grade button shows is now counted in study days whenever the word is due at a rollover, which every review is. Until now it was the clock gap -- hours below twenty-four, rounded days above -- so the same day read differently by the hour: at four in the afternoon tomorrow was "14h" and the day after was "2d", while at eight in the evening they were "10h" and "1d". Three buttons reading 14h / 14h / 14h looked like three buttons doing the same thing when underneath they were 1.5, 1.8 and 2.4 days with only whole days to say it in. Now the next rollover is "1d" and the one after is "2d" at any hour. A genuine learning step of minutes or hours is still said in minutes or hours. Display only -- nothing about the scheduling itself has changed, and nothing the September recalibration reads is touched.',
    'New: Settings has a button to reschedule the calendar to the current retention target. Changing the target only ever reached a word the next time you rated it; everything already on the calendar kept the gap the old target gave it. The button recomputes every review word\'s due date from what the scheduler already knows about it -- through the same formula and the same day snap a real rating uses, anchored on the word\'s own last review -- and changes nothing else: no rating, no stability, no difficulty, nothing in the log. It shows you the counts first (how many move sooner, how many later, how many are due at once) and does nothing until you confirm. It is built to be run once, after the September recalibration, not after every nudge of the slider, and it is not an undo button: the old dates are not kept, so reversing it means running it again at the old percentage.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.21.0',[
    'Fixed: two devices studying at the same time can no longer silently overwrite each other\'s synced copy. Until now an automatic push wrote this device\'s copy over whatever the sync server held without looking first -- so if another device had pushed in the meantime, its latest ratings vanished from the server until that device next synced, and anything reading the server in between (a third device, a restore) saw an incomplete history with no warning. An automatic push now looks at the synced copy first and, if another device has written to it since this one last merged, merges that in before writing. Nothing changes in the ordinary case: one look, then the same push as before, still paced to once a minute. It costs no extra writes -- a merge takes the place of the push it would have done -- and when the look finds the server already holds everything this device has, it saves a write outright. The final catch-up push when you put the app away is deliberately left exactly as it was: it is built to land even if the phone freezes the app a moment later, and the look-first step would sometimes cost it entirely, a commoner loss than the rare clash it would prevent.',
    'Fixed: a backup file with no deck marker is now refused. The marker is the only thing that tells a Latin backup from a Greek one -- the two decks\' card records look identical -- and a file without it used to load into whichever deck you restored it on, mapping every card onto the wrong words and then pushing that to sync as the winning copy. Every backup the app has saved since 16 August 2026 carries the marker; a genuinely older file needs re-exporting from a device that still holds the data. Sync is untouched either way: every synced copy has always carried the marker, and older copies are still read and merged as before.',
    'New: the front page has a download button beside the title that saves a dated backup file for every deck with data on this device, in one tap -- the same per-deck files the Export button in each deck\'s Settings writes, so restoring one works exactly as before. A copy old enough to predate the deck marker is stamped on its way out, so nothing this button saves can ever be refused by the fix above.',
    'Fixed: in the rare case where a sync merge fails outright and the app falls back to simply taking the synced copy, it now asks first if this device has settings changes that have not been pushed yet -- those were the one thing that fallback could silently discard. Reviews were never at risk there (the fallback only takes the synced copy when this device has rated nothing since it last agreed with it), and the question only appears when the settings actually differ.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.8',[
    'New: a word can now earn its way out of the set-aside pile. Drill it, and each study day you recall it correctly counts as one good day; at three, the app offers to bring it back. It only ever offers -- nothing leaves the pile on its own, and bringing a word back by hand works exactly as before. Three details are deliberate. Only the FIRST time you see a word each day counts: the drill already hands a missed word straight back for another go, and getting it right thirty seconds after being shown the answer is not the same as remembering it, so that second look earns nothing. Only one day can be banked per day, however many times you drill -- three recalls spread over three days are worth more than double three in one sitting, which is the whole reason the pile exists. And a day you get it wrong wipes the count back to zero, so "ready to come back" means the word is holding now, not that it once had three good days among twenty bad ones.',
    'New: the app tells you which words are ready in three places -- a line under the card the moment one crosses the mark mid-drill, a small tick beside it in both the set-aside list and the drill\'s own study list, and a count on the home screen line ("12 words set aside, 3 ready to come back").'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.7',[
    'Changed: a word\'s entry can no longer be edited. The "Save term" box that used to sit in a word\'s details popup -- letting you rewrite a built-in Latin or Greek headword -- is gone. On a prescribed list the word itself is the fact being memorised, not something to make easier by rewriting it, and the one field a rewrite could have legitimately helped with, the English meaning, was never editable in the first place, on purpose: it\'s what groups reverse-direction cards together, and changing it would silently reshuffle those groupings. A genuine typo belongs in the list itself, fixed once for everyone, not patched on one device. Deleting a word you added yourself is untouched -- that button is still there.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.6',[
    'New: words you set aside now come back properly. Until this build, bringing a word back out of the pile resumed it exactly where it stopped -- and where it stopped was the app judging it the hardest kind of word there is. That judgement never healed on its own: on your own fitted numbers, twenty-five correct answers in a row walk it back only from 9.97 out of ten to 9.61. What that meant in practice is that six perfect answers on a returned word left it on a three-day gap, where the same six answers on a word met fresh reach over a year. From now on it depends on what the word was doing when you set it aside. A word still being LEARNED comes back as new: the app drops what it thought it knew about how hard the word is and works it out again from your next answer. A word that was in real review is left exactly as it was -- that one has a genuine history worth keeping, and Anki\'s own advice is not to throw it away. Either way nothing you have ever pressed is lost: the full record of every answer stays untouched, and so do the counts of how many times you have seen the word.',
    'New: the set-aside pile now says it is there. One line on the home screen, showing how many words are in it and how long the oldest has been waiting; tapping it opens them. The pile has had a study screen of its own for weeks -- look-alike words grouped together, short focus sessions, its own statistics -- and no screen had ever mentioned it existed, so it had never once been opened.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.5',[
    'Changed: the app no longer sets any word aside on its own. It used to, after enough "Again" presses in a single day -- with a warning one press beforehand and a K key to override it. All of that is gone. The reason cuts against the obvious, so it is worth stating: the count it acted on was SAME-DAY failures, and same-day failures are exactly what Anki and SuperMemo both deliberately exclude when deciding a word is a problem. Failing a word four times in the ten minutes you are first learning it says the card is badly made -- too many meanings, a look-alike, no hook -- not that you cannot retain it. The app still counts, and still shows you the count. It just no longer acts on it. The decision about a word is yours.',
    'New: a second way out of a word, on the study card. Until now the only exit while studying was the flag, which takes a word out of review until you deliberately fetch it back -- the strongest thing available, offered at the moment you are most frustrated. Beside it there is now an hourglass, "Not today", which simply hides the word -- both directions -- until tomorrow, and moves on. It comes back on its own at the next rollover, with nothing for you to remember. These are Anki\'s own two exits, bury and suspend, and they carry Anki\'s own keys: "-" for Not today, "@" for Set aside. The app\'s older "F" still does the same as "@".',
    'Changed: those two keys work on any card, not only on a word that has already drawn two "Again" presses today. The icons still wait for that second Again -- two glyphs on every one of a thousand clean cards a day is chrome nobody reads -- but a deliberate keypress is never refused now. One rule instead of two: the keys always work, the icons appear when a word is fighting you. "F" also toasts now, on every card rather than only where the icon happened to be showing, because otherwise it would change whether a word stays in the deck with nothing at all on screen to say so.',
    'Fixed: a word could be both set aside and hidden-until-tomorrow at once, which meant bringing it back from the set-aside pile did not always bring it back -- it could vanish again for the rest of the day with nothing on screen to explain it. The two exits are now mutually exclusive, as they are in Anki: setting a word aside clears any "not today" on it, and bringing it back clears both, so a word that returns from the pile genuinely returns.',
    'Changed: the manual is rebuilt in two halves. Part One is a short read for someone meeting the app for the first time -- the one idea, the four buttons, the two rules everyone drifts from, the first week, and the two things to set up before anything else. Part Two is the ten-section reference to look things up in. It had grown into one long flow that tried to teach and to serve as reference at once and did neither, thickened with dates and build numbers from every release since July; those have gone to this changelog, where they belong.',
    'Fixed: every table in the manual ran off the side of the page -- a rule meant for columns of numbers was being applied to columns of prose, so paragraphs inside tables were laid out as single unbroken lines. On a phone the whole page scrolled sideways. The document was measured at 15,481 pixels wide against a 1,265-pixel window; it is now exactly as wide as the screen.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.4',[
    'New: the "Last card" panel now appears on a phone held upright too, as a single quiet line along the bottom of the card, just above the buttons: the word you just rated, what you pressed, and how far it jumped. Until now it existed only on a wide screen, so on a phone the only way to answer "what did I just do" was to undo. It never moves the word -- it sits in space that was empty anyway -- and it is placed at the bottom rather than the top because the top of the card is where the quick-rate flash plays.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.3',[
    'Fixed: on a wide screen in the Replace or Swap card shape, the "Last card" panel in the top-right corner could clip the tops of the last few letters of a long word -- on a phone held sideways, where the screen is short and the panel and the word were competing for the same band. It now takes less room on a short screen (the small "LAST CARD" caption above it goes; the word, the rating and the interval all stay), which lifts it clear of every word in the deck. Nothing changes on a laptop, an iPad, or a phone held upright.',
    'Fixed: a returning visit could load the new version of the app against an old cached copy of one of its shared files, and the study screen would then fail to draw a card at all. The two files both pages share are now stamped with the version number, so a browser can never pair a new page with an old copy of them again. If you ever saw a version of the app misbehave right after an update, this was why.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.2',[
    'New: a setting for the study screen\'s top bar ("Study screen, top bar", in Settings) -- keep the new/learning/due split it has always shown, switch to a single "N of today\'s total" progress line, or hide the counts entirely. Whichever you pick, an honest "~N min left" now sits alongside it, built from how long your last two weeks\' ratings actually took -- it says nothing rather than guess when there isn\'t enough recent timing to trust yet.',
    'New: the Done screen\'s recap grew from two lines to four -- this sitting\'s ratings and time, today\'s total and time, tomorrow\'s due count, and your streak when you have one -- so finishing a sitting says more than just what\'s due next.',
    'New: the Calibration panel\'s Diagnostics fold has a new row, "Faster than a glance" -- how many of your timed ratings revealed the answer in under 0.3s of seeing the question, too fast to have actually tried recalling first. Read a high share against "Time to recall" just above it: a low median pulled down by a lot of these is a different problem than genuinely fast recall.',
    'Fixed: the gear and flag icons (Settings, a word\'s Flag button, and every list row that shows one) could draw as a colour emoji instead of the app\'s own monochrome icon style, depending on the system font. They now force plain glyph rendering, the same fix the "about to set itself aside" warning icon already had.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.20.1',[
    'New: "Quick rate" is back in Settings, in one narrow form -- for a word you know the instant you see it, one press grades it Easy from the question side, with no reveal. On a keyboard that is the 4 key; on a phone it is a "Knew it" button that appears beside "Show answer". It was switched off for everyone on 2026.08.19.1 while the question of whether it should exist at all was settled properly against five days of your own ratings: on every day you studied at a keyboard, 43-70% of your Easy presses went through it. Off by default -- turn it on in Settings.',
    'Changed: it only ever means Easy now, on both devices. The old version graded 1, 2 and 3 from the question side too, and had a second "blind" mode that never showed the answer at all. Both are gone. Again before a reveal is pointless (you pressed it exactly zero times in about 1,900 presses), and Hard and Good are the two grades that mean "I actually looked at it" -- so they stay behind the reveal, and the keyboard and the phone now have one shortcut with one meaning between them rather than two different ones.',
    'New: the flash that follows a quick press now shows the grade it actually recorded, not just the answer. This matters because of the five-second rule: press "Knew it" slowly and the app records Good, and on a phone there was previously nothing on screen that said so. It now reads "Easy", or "Good -- that took a moment", under the answer.',
    'Fixed: on a wide screen using the Replace or Swap card shape, that flash was drawn straight on top of the next word, in the same slot, with no background of its own. It now sits in its own panel in the top-left corner, out of the card entirely -- the same treatment the recap card in the opposite corner already has. This was live in the app since those two shapes shipped and only invisible because quick rate was switched off.',
    'Improved (under the hood): every rating now also records whether the answer was revealed before you graded it. Nothing reads it yet. It exists so that September\'s scheduler fit can ask the one question this feature turns on -- whether a word you rated Easy without looking is really as well known as one you rated Easy after looking -- which your existing history cannot answer, because nothing recorded which was which.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.19.5',[
    'Changed: syncing between two copies of the app now MERGES them instead of making one replace the other. Until now, if you had studied on the phone and on the Home Screen icon (or a second device) before they synced, the app could only ask which copy to keep and lose the other side\'s ratings since they last agreed -- that cost 30-40 ratings, twice. Now both copies\' ratings are combined and nothing is lost to a sync: a word you rated on both sides keeps whichever rating came later (both stay in its history); a rating you undid stays undone; the day\'s counts are recounted from the combined ratings and study time is the sum of what each copy measured; each setting travels from whichever copy changed it more recently -- only "This device\'s name" stays local. It happens quietly, with a one-line summary afterwards ("Merged -- 12 reviews from iPhone · Safari, 30 from iPhone · Home Screen; 3 words rated on both, later rating kept"), kept under the sync code in Settings. "Pull now" has become "Pull and merge". The old either/or question only reappears if the merge itself fails, and Cancel still changes nothing. This is Anki\'s own shape -- the review log unions, everything else is last-writer-wins -- not a replay of history into card state; each merge also checks whether replaying both sides\' new ratings reproduces the intervals they recorded, and says so in the summary, for the day that replay is wanted.',
    'New: each copy of the app -- Safari, a Home Screen icon, another device -- now labels its own ratings with what it is (the typed device name plus "Home Screen", "Safari", "Chrome"...), so two copies both called "iPhone" never read as one again, and a merge can say where ratings came from. Under the hood every rating row gains one more column for this.',
    'New: a safety net, so any sync, merge or restore can be undone once. This device keeps the copy it last replaced -- "Restore the copy this replaced" in Settings brings it back (and the copy it displaces becomes the new spare). The sync server keeps one spare per deck -- the last copy pushed by a different copy of the app than the one that pushed last -- and "Restore the other copy\'s last push" fetches it. A restored copy wins on every device: its word states and settings take over at the next sync; no ratings are lost, the other copy\'s ratings merge back in. The server also tells this device if its clock is more than five minutes off, since sync orders ratings by the clock.',
    'New: a sitting log, and one tap for how it felt. The study clock now records each sitting -- when it started, when it stopped, how many ratings -- and once the queue runs out the Done screen asks "How did that feel? light / fine / heavy": one tap or none, stored as one answer for the day and shown on the calendar\'s day panel. Nothing reads the sittings yet; in a few weeks they let the stats page say whether words learnt in long sittings survive their first review as well as words from short ones, and whether your grading goes better at some hours than others.',
    'Fixed: the launcher page, when saving a device name or a new sync code, silently dropped the extraction key stored alongside them. It now writes back on top of what is there.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.19.4',[
    'New: a grade reminder, on by default and switchable off in Settings ("Grade reminder"), on a two-week trial. At the start of each sitting a one-line reminder of the rule -- Again is the only failing grade; Hard still moves the word on, it means "got it, with real effort"; not sure you recalled it? that is Again -- sits above the card until your first rating, the x, or leaving the screen, and its wording rotates between sittings so it does not fade into wallpaper. And once a study day, the first time a Hard passes a word still on its learning steps, a brief note says so at the moment it happens. Both are display only: nothing here changes what any button does, and there is no confirmation to tap through. Whether it is helping is a number, not a feeling: the Calibration panel\'s "By the button you pressed" row -- if Hard passes about as often as Good, Hard is being pressed as a mood rather than a judgement. Read it after the fortnight.',
    'New: the Calibration panel\'s Diagnostics now also split recall by the time of day you rated -- before noon, afternoon, evening, after 9 pm (a rating before 6 am counts as the night before) -- as the same spaced reviews against your target, plus the share of same-day learning-step presses that were not Again. Checked against the real history, late-night grading runs a long way below the morning; a lower figure late at night is the ordinary cost of tiredness, but it is also the least reliable data the scheduler gets, so if the gap is wide, keep new words for your sharpest hours and let review fill the rest.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.19.3',[
    'Changed: four rating buttons on every word, always the same four in the same order -- Again, Hard, Good, Easy -- and the number keys never move: 1 Again, 2 Hard, 3 Good, 4 Easy, whether the word is brand new, being relearned after a slip, or long settled. Until now Hard appeared only on settled words, and on a word still being learned the 2 and 3 keys meant Good and Easy instead. Once later stages mix learning with review, a row that changes shape and key meanings with the card is friction several hundred times a day, so the shifting is gone -- the buttons and their keys are as fixed as their labels.',
    'Changed: Hard is now a passing grade while a word is being learned or relearned. It advances the word a step, and from the last step it graduates -- exactly as Good does -- with the word marked harder, so it comes back sooner from then on. Every button except Again moves a word forward, so no word can ever get stuck circling the same short step: press Hard honestly twice on a new word and it graduates, just weaker. (This departs from Anki, where Hard on a learning step repeats the step -- an open request there since 2024, because with a single step an honest Hard can never graduate a card.) There is deliberately no "after so many Hards, do something" rule anywhere: that would only be needed if a button could fail to advance, and none can. Section 2 of the manual has the full button-by-state table.',
    'Changed (under the hood): a Hard pressed on a word already seen today no longer lowers the scheduler\'s belief in it. The scheduler\'s rule for a same-day repeat is that a pass never lowers a word\'s stability; this app had applied that rule to Good and Easy only, while every reference version of the scheduler applies it to Hard too. Harmless while Hard was review-only -- a settled word is rarely seen twice in a day -- but now that Hard is pressed in learning it would have fired every day and drifted from the engine the September parameter fit uses. One consequence worth knowing so it does not look like a bug: on the second same-day press, Hard and Good often show the same first interval. Hard\'s cost is the higher difficulty it leaves on the word, which shortens every later interval, not the first one.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.19.2',[
    'New: a Calibration panel on the Stats page -- is the scheduler keeping its promise? Every review it sets up is a small bet: it sends a word away for a stretch and predicts you will still know it when it comes back. The panel is how those bets came out, measured from your own ratings, not modelled, for whichever direction is switched on at the top of the page, over all time or the last 30 or 14 days. Two readings of the same evidence. "By the interval you were given": for words sent away 1-6, 7-13, 14-20, 21-29, 30-44 and 45-plus study days, how often you still knew them when they came back, each bar drawn against the mark of your own target retention and amber where it falls short, with a total row underneath. "By the button you pressed": for Hard, Good and Easy, how often the word was still known next time round -- read the rows against each other; if Hard passes about as often as Good, your Hard means Good, and if Easy passes no more often than Good, the longer interval Easy buys is not being earned. Again never sets an interval (the word comes back within minutes), so it has nothing to test and the panel says so rather than showing a number. Two honesty rules, deliberately: a bucket nothing has reached yet reads "not enough history yet", never 0% or an empty bar -- the longer intervals are unreachable until a word has actually been sent that far and come back, and an empty row is not a failure -- and under twenty reviews the raw count is shown instead of a percentage. Folded away underneath, "Diagnostics", not for everyday use: the one rule that changes what you pressed (a slow Easy recorded as Good) with how often it has actually fired, and a line saying it is the only one -- if a second line ever appears there, something has been put back between the button and the scheduler; how you have been pressing, week by week, as one stacked bar per week (Easy should not outnumber Good); recall by whether you reviewed on time, early, late or very late; and the share of same-day repeats, the lapse rate and your median time to recall.',
    'New: a one-line reminder on the front screen from 2 September 2026 -- two weeks after the drill layer was removed -- until you tap it. Tapping opens the Calibration panel and clears the line; the panel\'s own "Remind me to look again in two weeks" sets it up again for the next checkpoint. The fortnight matters: only ratings made after the change say anything about how the scheduler behaves now, and this is what makes sure it gets looked at rather than left to memory.',
    'Fixed: the front screen\'s "How well the model is predicting you" fold was quietly leaving out every one-day interval. A word sent to "tomorrow" is granted whatever is left of today plus the night -- sixteen hours if you rated it at two in the afternoon -- and the old test read anything under twenty-four hours on the clock as a same-day step. On a real history that dropped about two-thirds of the spaced reviews, and not a random two-thirds: the one-day reviews are the ones that go best, so the figure was both a third of the sample and lower than the truth (measured on one deck: 88% of 8,900 reviews once counted properly, against 84% of 1,900 before). It now counts study days, the same way the scheduler itself does, and the new Calibration panel counts exactly the same reviews it does.',
    'Improved (under the hood): each rating now also records the button you actually pressed, alongside the grade the scheduler was given. They differ only when a slow Easy is recorded as Good, and that is precisely what could not be told apart before -- a Good logged after six seconds might have been a real Good or a converted Easy -- so the rule\'s fire rate on the Calibration panel is counted from this build on, not guessed at from the earlier history. Older ratings simply lack the column; nothing is rewritten.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.19.1',[
    'Removed: the "drill" layer that sat on top of the scheduler -- "Clearing a word for the day" (Only Easy clears it / Good is enough), "Same-day bounces before it clears anyway" and "Consecutive good days before release" are gone from Settings and from the code, not just switched off. What they did: after you pressed Good or Hard on a word still being learned, the app ignored the interval the scheduler had just worked out and handed back a flat 10 minutes instead, until an Easy; the days-in-a-row setting dragged review words back into that too. That is where the every-10-minutes trap the last build fixed had lived, and it is also why so many intervals ended up being set by an Easy press -- Easy was the only way out. Deleted rather than repaired: the two learning steps (1 minute, then 10 minutes, then graduate) already give a new word its same-day survival check, and the drill layer had been getting in the way of them. Any old value these three settings still carry in a saved backup or on another device is ignored from now on -- nothing to do, nothing to re-set.',
    'Removed: "Weekly review day". It nudged every interval of 3 to 21 days onto one weekday, by up to three days either way -- which, at the 3 to 7 day intervals most words actually live at, meant Hard, Good and Easy could all land on the very same date and the buttons stopped meaning different things. Reviews are spread across the week again, as the scheduler chooses. An old value is likewise ignored.',
    'Changed: "Longest interval" is shown in Advanced settings but no longer editable -- it reads out the ceiling (36500 days, a hundred years, so effectively none) rather than offering a dial that was never meant to be turned.',
    'Removed from Settings: "Quick rate" -- grading a settled word straight from the question with 1/3 (or 1/4), no tap to reveal first. Off for everyone now, and no saved value can turn it back on. It graded honestly, but with no reveal the only sign that a slow Easy was recorded as Good is a brief toast -- the flash shows the answer, never the grade -- and it kept the card shape, notes and the Hard button dark. It may return once that is designed properly.',
    'The learning steps themselves are unchanged: Good on a brand-new word brings it back in 10 minutes, and a second Good then graduates it -- as it was always meant to; the drill layer had been holding it there for an Easy. Again still sends a new word back to 1 minute; a lapsed review word still gets one 10-minute relearn step. On a review word, Hard, Good and Easy are usually three different intervals again (at very short intervals they can still round to the same day -- that is the scheduler counting in whole days, not the old nudge).',
    'Improved (under the hood): every rating now also records what the app believed about the word at that moment -- whether it was new, in review or relearning, and how stable it thought the memory was -- plus the state it wrote afterwards. Old ratings simply lack these columns; nothing is rewritten. This is what lets the calibration work planned for the coming weeks read your real numbers directly instead of reconstructing them.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.18.2',[
    'Fixed: a word could get stuck repeating every 10 minutes forever with no way out, if "Consecutive good days before release" was set above zero. The escape that\'s supposed to release a word after enough same-day tries without an Easy ("Same-day bounces before it clears anyway") only ever recognised a word still being freshly learned -- so a word held by the days-in-a-row setting alone, after it had already graduated past new-word learning, never qualified and just kept bouncing. It now escapes the same way a fresh word already did.',
    'Fixed: the sync conflict prompt could name both copies the same way when two devices shared a device name -- "This device (iPhone) has 5 reviews... OK = take the copy from iPhone" left no way to tell which iPhone was which. When both sides share a name, each is now tagged too -- "iPhone (this device)" against "iPhone (the synced copy, pushed 14m ago)" -- and which copy OK takes is stated on its own line.',
    'Fixed: on a wide screen using the Replace or Swap study card shape, the small stats chips under the word (reviewed count, streak, last rating -- shown when the study screen isn\'t set to Simplified) could land directly on the revealed answer instead of below it. They now sit in their own row, the same as they always have under the default two-pane layout.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.18.1',[
    'New: a word list can be imported with no key and no model call at all -- Settings, "Import a word list". Paste text or open a file (.txt/.csv/.tsv/.json): columns copied from a spreadsheet, a Quizlet or Anki export, "term -- gloss" or "term: gloss" lines, a numbered list, chapter headings that become the section. It is read entirely on this device, and a preview shows every word found and every line skipped, with the reason, before anything is kept -- change the separator, swap the two sides, or say which column is which, then Accept all, review only the rows it wasn\'t sure of one at a time, or triage everything as before. The words can start a new deck or be added to the deck you\'re in. The model-based extraction is still there, folded underneath as the option for a passage of prose.',
    'New: a PDF can be opened straight on the import screen (Settings, "Import a word list"). A printed vocabulary list sets its columns at tab stops, and copying that page turns the gaps into single spaces -- the words are read here by position instead, so the columns come back separated. Every column of a real table is kept and its header row names them (so an exam board\'s "Meaning(s)" column becomes the gloss, not a column that gets thrown away); a page printed two-up is read down one column and then the other, rather than across; page numbers, repeated table headers and front matter are left out; a definition that wrapped is rejoined; part headings still become sections. The freshers\' Latin list reads as 3012 of its 3038 lines this way, against none at all from a copy of the same pages, and a 500-word SAT list printed two-up comes through complete. A scan or a photograph has no text in it to read -- let the phone read that one and paste the words in.',
    'New: a Word document or Excel workbook can be opened straight on the import screen too, alongside the PDF and plain-text paths. A Word document\'s own table is read as columns, the same way a PDF\'s or a pasted spreadsheet\'s are -- or, if it has no table, its paragraphs are read the ordinary way, one line each. An Excel or Numbers workbook\'s first sheet comes in the same way a paste from one already does, its header row naming the columns.',
    'New: the import preview now says what it cannot show you, and warns about what it cannot tell you. A long import is sampled in three windows -- the start, the middle and the end -- instead of only the first 400 rows, because the top of a list is where the front matter is while a column boundary that drifts does so somewhere in the middle. Above the buttons, a line saying what a parser gets wrong it gets wrong quietly, and that a word learnt wrong is worse than one never imported. And for a page this cannot read at all -- a scan, a photograph -- "Copy a prompt for an AI" puts an instruction on the clipboard for a chat assistant: give it the page, read what comes back against the page, bring that text back here. The prompt forbids inventing, translating or correcting anything and makes it declare whatever it could not read, because this parser fails loudly and a model fails quietly. The manual (section 8) prints the same prompt.',
    'New: a new deck now has a kind -- language, word list, or formula sheet -- which sets what its two directions are called ("Word → Definition", "Name → Formula") instead of always "… → English".',
    'New: a deck created on this device can be saved as a deck file (Settings, "Export deck file") and imported through the same import screen on another device, keeping the same words and ids so a restored backup re-attaches its history to them.',
    'New: a Hard button, on words that have settled into a normal rhythm. It means "I got it, but that was a real struggle" -- a pass, but a weak one, scheduled sooner than Good and nothing like Again. It sits between Again and Good, and takes the 2 key with it: the numbers now run left to right along the buttons, so a settled word is 1 Again, 2 Hard, 3 Good, 4 Easy. A word still being learned keeps the three buttons it always had, on 1/2/3 -- there, the gap between Hard and Good is a few minutes either way and not worth the decision. Every button prints its own number, so you never have to remember which shape you are in.',
    'New: two more ways to lay out the study card on a wide screen, alongside the existing two-pane spread -- Settings, "Study card shape". Replace puts the answer exactly where the word just was instead of beside it; Swap does the same but keeps the word too, shrunk to a small line above, so the two are still both there to compare. Either way nothing moves or resizes on reveal -- only what the card is showing changes. The recap card in the corner -- what you rated last time and how long ago -- looks and sits exactly as it always has, in all three shapes. The two-pane spread stays the default; nothing changes here until one of the other two is chosen. Pairs naturally with Quick rate for the fastest pace the app offers. A phone held upright is unaffected either way, same as the wide layout itself.',
    'New: Shift+Z always undoes the last rating, no exceptions. Z alone (or 0) still corrects a word to Again when pressed right after a "quick rate" grade, rather than undoing it -- genuinely the more useful reading of that keystroke most of the time, fixing a fat-fingered 1/2/3 in one press -- but that left no way to get a plain undo in that same moment if correcting to Again wasn\'t actually what you meant. Shift+Z is that way, always.',
    'New: the manual (Settings, "How this works") has a full keyboard-shortcut reference -- one table per screen, Study/Drill/reviewing an import -- instead of shortcuts only ever mentioned in passing.',
    'Changed: the I/O/P right-hand alternative to 1/2/3 is gone, everywhere it existed -- rating, drill honour rounds and import triage. It was there for whichever hand you preferred; it went unused, and keeping it would have meant inventing a fourth letter for Hard.',
    'Changed: the timing rule is now one rule -- take more than five seconds and an Easy is recorded as Good. It used to also record a slow Good as a weaker pass; that is gone, because Hard is now a button you press yourself rather than something the clock guesses at. Good and Hard are left exactly as you press them, however long you take. Nothing is ever recorded higher than what you pressed, and Again is still never altered.',
    'Changed: the five-second line replaces the old ten-second one. Checked against real review history, ten seconds turned out never to fire at all -- a genuinely effortless answer arrives in about two seconds, and almost none take longer than seven, so the old line sat past the end of the range it was meant to catch. Five seconds catches the slow ones without touching ordinary fast answers.',
    'Changed: a gloss or note can now be up to 500 characters (was 200) -- a definition or an example sentence no longer gets cut short. Terms stay at 200.',
    'Fixed: the rating buttons were hard to read in several themes. Across the twelve palettes, 31 of the button labels sat below the standard contrast bar against their own button -- Catppuccin light\'s Good was the worst at less than half the required ratio. Every one now clears it. The fix moves only the label colour (or, for two dark themes, deepens the button behind it), never the hue, so each theme still looks like itself: Gruvbox\'s red is still Gruvbox\'s red. This was long-standing and unrelated to the new Hard button -- it turned up during a check that Hard\'s own colours were safe.',
    'Fixed: importing a word list copied from a PDF could silently drop or misread entries in a few common cases -- a Greek deck using a lookalike character for the letter mu, a grammatical tag like "(+ gen.)" getting separated from its word, a bare numeral heading not being recognised as a section title, and a trailing page number leaking into the previous line\'s meaning. Checked against two real vocabulary lists, 64 and 22 pages: both now come in essentially complete.',
    'Fixed: a line separated by "--" (two hyphens, typed where there is no real dash key) was not recognised as a separator at all. It is now, ranked alongside the real em and en dashes.',
    'Fixed: the paste box on the import screen was a fraction of the width of every other field on that screen, and far too short -- both from the same missing style rule, not a setting anyone had chosen. It now matches the width of the fields above it and starts noticeably taller, with a visible handle to make it taller still.',
    'Fixed: a note typed while triaging words for a NEW deck was collected and then silently dropped when the deck was created; it now lands on that word the first time the new deck opens.',
    'Fixed: a freshly generated sync code could look ready before it actually was. Settings\' "Get a code for this device" pushed straight away, but showed the new code as a plain, copyable value before that push had resolved -- copy it and switch devices in the gap and the push could still be mid-flight when the tab closed. The launcher\'s own "Get a code for this device" was worse: it only ever minted a code and asked you to open a deck afterward to actually push anything, so switching devices without doing that first meant nothing ever reached the server. The launcher now pushes both decks itself, and both screens disable the code field and button until the push is actually confirmed, not just started.',
    'Fixed: a sync push that failed used to read identically, in Settings and on the front screen, to one that had simply never been attempted -- "nothing synced yet" either way. A real failure now says so, with the reason, and keeps saying so until the next attempt succeeds.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.17.3',[
    'New: notes are no longer just for set-aside words -- any word can carry one, shown on the study card once you\'ve answered it (a new "Notes on the study card" setting, on by default), and edited from Browse.',
    'New: Browse\'s rows get an "i" button -- a proper detail view for any word: how it\'s going, its note, and, for a word you\'ve added yourself, editing its text or deleting it outright. Tapping the row itself still does the old quick reveal-the-gloss-and-stats, unchanged, for a fast glance down a list; the "i" button is the deliberate way in for anything more.',
    'New: words can be added to a deck by hand -- Browse\'s own "+" button, term/gloss/section/an optional note. A typo in any word\'s term, built-in or one you\'ve added, can be corrected from its own detail view; the meaning side stays fixed everywhere, since editing it could silently change which reverse-direction cards exist.',
    'New: a whole new deck can be built from a passage of text, a word list, or a page\'s own URL -- Settings, "Create a deck from text". Every candidate is reviewed one at a time, kept or binned, editable first -- nothing becomes part of a deck unapproved -- and what you keep becomes a real deck, indistinguishable from Latin or Greek once it exists, right down to the launcher and its own study days. The real extraction call needs one setup step first (Settings, "Extraction key", then pasting that value into the sync Worker by hand); until that\'s done, the same screen still accepts a hand-typed word list directly, useful on its own for trying the review screen out.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.17.2',[
    'Fixed: the Home screen\'s own heading read "Vocabula" (Latin) or "Λέξεις" (Greek) instead of the deck\'s name -- a thematic label left over from before the two decks shared one engine. It now reads the same "Latin"/"Greek" already used for the browser tab and the pinned Home Screen icon, instead of a third name that could drift from those two.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.17.1',[
    'New: the "Set aside" list on the front screen shows each word\'s meaning beside it, so the pile can be read over before drilling without going to Browse for the definitions.',
    'Changed: a deck\'s words are now loaded after the page rather than as part of it. Invisible when it works; when it doesn\'t -- a flaky connection, a half-finished upload -- the page says so and offers a way back to the launcher, instead of an app skeleton that silently never fills in. It is also the piece a deck built on the device itself would need.',
    'Changed: the calendar\'s colours now scale against the same daily average the stat chip above them shows. They were scaled against your all-time average while the chip showed whichever window you had picked, so on a history that changed pace the two disagreed by a factor of three -- a day the chip called ordinary could render nearly empty. Moving the window now moves both together.',
    'Fixed: the front screen invented a pace you were not running. With reviews continuing but no new words introduced for a fortnight -- which is exactly what pausing one direction looks like -- it reported "your recent pace of 1 a day" and promised an arrival date built on it. A true zero now reads as zero, and the line says which zero it is: a paused direction is told the allowance is sitting there unused, an allowance genuinely set to none is told to raise it. That second message had been unreachable for anyone who had studied at all.',
    'Fixed: an arrival date outside the current year now says the year. At a slow pace against a whole list it could read "September 13" for a date in 2055.',
    'Fixed: "Words met so far" disagreed with the by-part table directly underneath it. It had quietly stopped counting words you had set aside, while the table below and the status split beside it both kept counting them -- 272 against 300, on a real check. A word you have met and then parked is still a word you have met; the projection that wants the narrower count now asks for it separately.',
    'Fixed: the launcher\'s per-deck line mixed two different days. The "done" count came from the last time that deck saved, while the time beside it came from today, so a deck last opened yesterday could read "12 due, 90 done, 0m" -- yesterday\'s reviews next to today\'s zero minutes. Today\'s number now comes from today\'s own record. A day with no study reads 0m rather than "under a minute".',
    'Fixed: the combined streak on the launcher ignored a deck\'s own streak-start date while the per-deck figures printed beside it applied it, so the headline could contradict its own breakdown -- "19-day streak (Latin 4)".',
    'Removed: "Learning at once" and "Unlock the reverse direction after". Both had been switched off by default, both overlapped what the daily new-word limit already does, and between them they carried nine settings and a suggestion feature that never became relevant. Nothing about scheduling changes -- checked by replaying 9,586 real reviews through both versions and getting identical results.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.9',[
    'New: the launcher shows what to actually do. Each deck\'s card now names how many words are ready right now, plus a one-line readout of today specifically -- due, done, and time spent, with a rough time-to-finish once there\'s enough of a pace to estimate from. The old 35-day dot-strip on each card is gone in favour of it; a new Activity section below shows a combined study streak across both decks instead, plus a scrolling calendar for each -- one shared timeline, so a gap in one deck is easy to see against the other.',
    'New: either deck can be shown or hidden from that calendar with a tap, without changing anything it tracks.',
    'Changed: the launcher reads its list of decks from one small file instead of Latin and Greek being written out twice, once for the deck cards and once for the calendar. Invisible today -- means a future deck needs one file added, not an edit in two places.',
    'Fixed: two long-standing gaps in the launcher\'s own security policy, found while building the above -- the small arrow on its dropdown menu was silently blocked from loading (cosmetic only), and the version number at the foot of the page was never actually able to double-check itself against the real build number, quietly relying on being kept in step by hand instead.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.8',[
    'Changed: the theme roster. Monokai is gone — it sat one shade off Dracula with nothing left to tell them apart. 8-Bit is gone too, along with the legibility problem noted in its own write-up (its light mode\'s "82 learning" was, measured, close to invisible against the authentic Game Boy screen — fixing that meant giving up the exact hardware colours that were the point of it). Rubric\'s laid-paper look is retired in favour of a new Roman register: gold leaf on white marble in light mode, the same gold on black marble in dark, renamed Marble to match. Neon\'s dark mode is a shade lighter than before — a true near-black canvas read as oppressive rather than moody, not atmospheric. New: Wood, honey oak and brushed brass in light mode, dark walnut and the same brass in dark. Twelve themes in all, same count as before the two arrivals five days ago briefly made it thirteen.',
    'New: the theme picker shows more of what each theme actually looks like. Every card in the grid now carries a small dot legend — the exact colours Again, Good and Easy will be in that theme — below its light/dark preview, once per card rather than duplicated across both halves. Above the grid, a full-size preview of whichever theme is currently selected mocks up the real study card split straight down the middle: the left half lit as that theme\'s light mode, the right half its dark, so both can be judged side by side before committing, at the size the actual card uses rather than a thumbnail.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.7',[
    'Changed: the two decks are now one page. Latin opens as deck.html?deck=latin and Greek as deck.html?deck=greek -- the same engine, with each deck\'s word list in its own small file -- so a fix ships once instead of twice. The old latin.html and greek.html addresses are gone: a Home Screen icon added before today needs removing and adding again from the launcher\'s links. Nothing on the device itself moves.',
    'Changed: sync no longer uses a GitHub gist. One short sync code (ten letters and digits) replaces the gist id and token: get one on the first device, type the same code into the other, and both decks on every device stay in step. The code never leaves the device -- only a lookup key and an encrypted copy reach the sync server, which cannot read them -- and the copy is compressed before it goes, so a push is a fraction of the size it was. Pushes are paced to at most one a minute during a sitting, plus one more when the app is put away. The old gist token is deleted from this device.',
    'Changed: how the app saves. The review log -- the one part of your data that grows without limit -- is now only ever added to, never rewritten, and everything else is saved as one bounded record; the browser\'s smaller store holds only a tiny boot record. Saving a rating stays quick however long the log grows. For now the old whole-copy record is still written alongside, as insurance for a few weeks. The app also asks the browser to mark its storage persistent on every launch.',
    'Changed: every word now carries a permanent id, so words can later be added, removed or reordered without a word\'s history ever attaching to its neighbour. Today\'s ids are exactly the old positions, checked word by word against real backups under both the old and the new engine, so nothing on any device changed meaning. A history whose word has left the list is kept parked, never deleted, and comes back if the word does.',
    'New: the word list\'s search box matches both sides of every card -- a term finds its row from the English direction and a gloss from the other -- ignoring accents, breathings, macrons and the final-sigma difference.',
    'New: in the Greek deck, the word list\'s search box and Drill\'s typed English-to-Greek answer both take Greek from an ordinary Latin keyboard, on the Hoplite keyboard\'s own scheme (a gives α, u θ, j ξ, c ψ, v ω, w final ς; digits 1-9 add or remove breathings, accents, macron, breve, iota subscript, diaeresis on the letter just typed). The αβγ/abc button beside each box switches it off for typing English; the phone\'s own Greek keyboard is left alone either way.',
    'Fixed: the launcher\'s deck cards did not reflect a deck\'s own theme once it was on one of the five newest themes (the launcher carried a stale hand-copied colour table), and its 35-day strip went transparent in the Retention colouring (one colour it mixed was undefined there). Both pages now read one shared palettes file, and the strip has every colour it needs.',
    'Fixed: opening deck.html with no or an unknown ?deck= showed the whole empty app underneath a bare error line; it is now a proper stop with a link back to the launcher.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.6',[
    'New: Glass and Neon get their own material now, instead of no texture at all. Glass leans into an actual "Liquid Glass" look -- its cards are genuinely translucent and frosted (a real background blur, not a flat tint), with a thin bright edge catching the light along their top like glass would. Neon leans into an actual glow -- rating labels, the front screen\'s counts, the main Study button and a few others now radiate softly in their own colour, brighter and further-reaching in dark mode where a glow has somewhere dark to bloom into, softer in light mode where the same glow would otherwise look like a smudge.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.5',[
    'New: manual confusable groups. The Word list\'s Set-aside filter now lets you pick several set-aside words yourself and group them as confusable, for whatever reason actually confuses you -- spelling, meaning, meeting them on the same page -- not just the two-words-that-happen-to-look-alike pairing Drill\'s contrastive round could otherwise ever find on its own. Select two or more, tap Group, and give it an optional label; the same contrastive round then quizzes the whole group against each other. Add one more word to an existing group later from its own row, or remove one -- a group left with fewer than two words is dissolved on its own, nothing to tidy up by hand.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.4',[
    'Changed: the app icon. The coral border now reaches every edge of the icon -- it used to leave a thin gap of the cream background between itself and the true edge on three of its four corners (hidden next to the digit badge in the fourth, visible everywhere else). That badge -- a small number in the corner counting the build -- is gone entirely, and the V itself is now a plain brushed silver rather than ink with gold serifs.',
    'New: three of yesterday\'s five new themes get a faint background texture. Rubric gets laid-paper fibres, Steel gets brushed-metal streaks, and 8-Bit gets the Game Boy\'s own visible pixel grid. Glass and Neon don\'t -- both are built around a smooth surface (translucency, glow), and a texture would work against exactly that.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.3',[
    'New: the launcher page can now check the gist directly for the latest palette and study-day data, instead of only ever showing whatever this device last saved. Previously the two cards there (each one\'s border colour, its 35-day strip) only ever reflected this browser\'s own last-known copy of that deck -- correct once the deck had actually been opened here, but stuck at whatever it showed before that if a palette change or a study session happened on another device. It now checks the gist itself once a minute at most (one request covers both decks at once), and a "Refresh" button beside the two cards forces an immediate check regardless of that limit. Still never writes anything back to either deck\'s own saved data -- exactly as read-only as before, just no longer blind to what hasn\'t been opened on this device yet.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.2',[
    'Changed: Kanagawa and Everforest are retired from the theme picker. Measured rather than guessed: both sat inside the same nine-degree, pale-yellow-gold hue band as Vellum and Gruvbox, and Gruvbox and Everforest\'s green rating colour were near-identical hexes. Gruvbox stays as that family\'s one theme -- the best-known name of the three, the most saturated, and the only fully colourless dark mode among all of them.',
    'New: five themes. Rubric, a regal cousin to Vellum built from real illuminated-manuscript history -- true rubrication red and gold leaf on a warmer parchment, instead of Vellum\'s muted terracotta. Steel and Glass, taking their material cues from two of Chess.com\'s actual board skins (sampled from the real files, not guessed): brushed gunmetal with an oxide-copper accent, and cool slate with the same bevel-highlight trick the real glass board uses in place of true transparency. Neon, glow-on-black -- knowingly built after the popular neon convention rather than Chess.com\'s own Neon set, which turned out to be a soft pastel with no glow in it at all. 8-Bit, whose light mode is the real four-shade Game Boy screen, down to the exact original hardware hex values.',
    'Changed: the theme picker cards are smaller. Each one dropped the three-colour again/good/easy strip from both halves -- it mostly repeated itself, since the same three colours light both halves of one card -- and kept the word sample and the accent bar. Three themes fit per row now instead of two (four on wider screens), which matters more with thirteen themes on the list than it did with ten.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.16.1',[
    'Removed: the front screen\'s \u201cHit \u2018Again\u2019 5+ times today\u201d list. It was built to catch a sitting going wrong in real time, which is a problem you have while taking on a lot of new words; it stops earning its place once study is mostly review. Removing it also fixed a real fault, since the count beside that heading was never counting what the heading said: it was the number of words on the list, and the list also held every word already set aside, so it could read "5+ times today (15)" above fifteen words that had not been answered wrong once that day. The section is now simply "Set aside", and says what it holds. Nothing about how a word gets set aside has changed \u2014 neither the threshold that does it automatically nor the much lower one that lets you flag a word by hand \u2014 and the Drill still works from exactly the same pile.',
    'Changed: Settings is roughly a third of its old length. Twelve scheduler settings \u2014 the learning-at-once cap, longest interval, when a study day begins, the reverse-direction gate, hesitation adjustment, the two drill-hold settings, consecutive good days, weekly review day, the head start for known words, unseen-before-shaky, and the automatic set-aside threshold \u2014 now sit behind one control at the bottom of the page. Everything is still there and still does exactly what it did; the page just no longer opens with all of it at once. The two per-direction new-word limits share one field, the device name moved in beside the gist it labels, and several of the longer explanations have been cut to a line.',
    'Changed: the three separate "study screen, wide layout" switches are now one. Review history, keyboard hints and animation are all on by default, and a single Simplified setting turns all three off together. The last card you rated is now always shown, whichever you pick \u2014 it was previously tied to the review-history switch.',
    'Changed: four defaults, for a fresh install only \u2014 nothing on a device already in use moves. A study day now begins at 6am rather than 4am; the reverse direction is unlocked from the start rather than after fourteen days; the experimental learning-at-once cap starts off; and the three study-screen extras start on.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.15.6',[
    'Changed: the calendar no longer prints three small figures inside each day. They were unreadable by construction -- the cell is filled with a colour scaled to that day, and the three numbers kept fixed colours chosen to sit on the page background instead. Measured across every theme and both light and dark, between 48 and 63 of the 86 figures on screen fell below a legible contrast, and every single theme had at least one number the exact same brightness as the square behind it -- invisible, not merely faint. The colour of the square already says how busy the day was, and tapping any day still gives all four figures at a readable size. The day numbers themselves are now measured against whatever colour the square actually ended up, so they stay legible in every theme: nothing on the calendar now falls below a 3.7:1 contrast, against a previous worst of 1.0:1.',
    'Changed: the calendar\'s colours are blended in a different colour space, which matters most for the Retention view. Mixing green into red the old way passed through a muddy brown, so two days at genuinely different recall could read as "murky green" and "lush green" with no way to tell which was better; it now runs red through orange and yellow into green, the way a traffic light does, which reads as an order at a glance.',
    'New: a colour scale under the calendar, labelled at both ends, so there is no guessing which end is which. It is drawn from the same two colours as the squares themselves, so a square can be matched against it by eye.',
    'Changed: the theme picker now shows every theme, in both light and dark at once, instead of a list of names. Each card is a miniature of the study screen -- a real word from this deck in the reading font, the three rating buttons, the accent -- so a theme can be judged before it is chosen, and its dark mode judged without switching to dark mode first. Tapping a card applies it immediately.',
    'Changed: this deck now keeps its reviews in the browser\'s larger store rather than its small one. The small one holds about 5 MB and a full deck was heading for that within a year and a half at the present rate; the larger one is not meaningfully limited. Both stores were always being written and the app always preferred whichever held more, so nothing changes about your data -- what changes is that running out of room in the small one is now reported as the ordinary thing it is, instead of "this device is refusing to save. Check that Safari is not in Private Browsing", which was alarming, wrong about the cause, and untrue while the other store was working perfectly.',
    'New: the gist backup now reports its own size, on a successful push and on a failed one. The backup is sent as a single file and had no idea how large it was, so a push that failed because it had simply grown too big looked exactly like a push that failed for any other reason. The front screen also says so once the backup passes a megabyte.',
    'Changed: the app icon. The two flat bars at the top of the V are now gold; the rest of the letterform stays as it was. The small corner badge counts in Roman numerals, and shrinks to fit -- VIII is four characters wide and would have overflowed the corner at the old fixed size. Zero is written N, for nulla, there being no Roman numeral for it.',
  ]],
  ['2026.08.15.5',[
    'New: Drill now opens with a choice every time — check the whole set-aside pile in one pass, same as before, or run a shorter focus session (10-25 words, picked with a slider) instead. A focus session rotates through whichever words haven\'t been drilled recently, so repeated short sessions still reach the whole pile eventually rather than always landing on the same handful.',
    'Changed: drilling a Greek word back to its English meaning is a plain reveal-then-judge-yourself flashcard again, not a typed answer — typing out the English gloss by hand wasn\'t testing anything a reveal doesn\'t already, and just slowed things down. English to Greek is unchanged, still typed — producing the actual Greek word is the point there. Confusable-pair matching rounds are unaffected either direction.',
    'New: a one-line note can be attached to any set-aside word — why it keeps getting confused, a mnemonic, anything worth remembering next time you meet it. Add or edit one from the front screen\'s Set-aside list, or from Drill\'s own list view, where the note also shows back read-only — never during an actual practice round, so it can\'t turn a retrieval test into an open-book one.',
    'New: a Drill section on the Stats screen — how many words are currently set aside, the oldest one\'s age, how many have never actually been drilled yet or have a note, sessions run and their first-look accuracy, and how the pile has moved over the last two weeks.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.15.4',[
    'New: a word about to set itself aside now warns you first, and you can overrule it. Once a word is one \"Again\" away from the threshold in Settings, a small amber warning mark appears on the study card beside the flag; tap it, or press K, and that word is kept in for the rest of the study day \u2014 the failure that would have parked it no longer does. It exists because the count cannot tell two very different situations apart: sometimes a word simply will not come, but sometimes you have three of its four meanings and being cast out of the deck is the last thing it needs. The override lasts the study day and no further, deliberately \u2014 the count that triggers a set-aside is itself same-day, so tomorrow starts at zero and the warning is live again; a word you find yourself rescuing several days running is one the app is probably right about. Press it a second time to let the word go after all \u2014 and if it is already past the threshold by then, it is set aside there and then rather than quietly later. Words you have already set aside are untouched by any of this: the flag is still what brings those back.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.15.3',[
    'Fixed: with gist backup switched on, some devices were contacting GitHub on every single rating instead of once every few seconds. This happened whenever a device that already had review history was pointed at a gist holding fewer reviews than it -- and also whenever the token had expired, where every rating fired another doomed request. Either way it burned through GitHub\'s hourly request limit and caused the "push failed" toasts; the fix from yesterday only closed one of the two ways in.',
    'Fixed: undoing a rating also erased the time you had spent on that card, so "studied today" and the Stats time figures quietly drifted below the stopwatch on the study screen, which never rolled back. The rating itself still reverts in full -- only the minutes you genuinely sat there are now kept. This mattered more than it used to, because the new 0/Z quick-rate correction runs an undo every time you use it.',
    'Fixed: the 0/Z shortcut that corrects a quick-rate press to "Again" could fire far later than intended. If you quick-rated the last card, waited on the "Almost there" screen, and pressed 0 or Z once a card came back, it marked the previous, no-longer-visible word Again -- adding a lapse -- instead of undoing. It is now strictly the very next keypress, as described, and going back to the front screen in between cancels it. Used normally, straight after a quick-rate press, it is unchanged.',
    'Fixed: the calendar gear\'s "streak start date" could show the day before the one actually saved, and re-saving what it showed walked the date a further day back each time. Only affected people whose study day begins at midnight.',
    'Fixed: two places still said a word is set aside after eight "Again" presses in a day. That number has been your own adjustable setting since last update -- both now show whatever it is actually set to.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.15.2',[
    'Quick rate is noticeably faster to click through: 1/I and 3/P (and, in "flash the answer" mode, 2/O too) now grade a word and move straight to the next one\'s front immediately, instead of waiting out the flash first. "Flash the answer" still shows you the answer right after you grade -- it just floats over the next word as a brief receipt now, rather than holding it up. Quick rate also now applies to a word mid its short post-lapse relearn step, not only to a fully settled review -- previously it dropped back to reveal-then-rate for those. New: pressing 0 or Z right after a quick-rate press grades that same word Again instead of undoing it -- one keystroke to fix a mis-press (a 3 that should have been a 1, say) without losing your place or having to look at the card again; any other 0/Z is still a normal undo. New and still-learning words are unaffected by any of this -- reveal-then-rate stays the only route there, same as always.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.15.1',[
    'The "Study days" calendar is substantially richer: cells are now colour-graded by how much you studied that day (or, toggled to "Retention", by how well you recalled it) instead of a plain did/didn\'t dot, scaled to your own average rather than a fixed number. A new stat row adds longest streak and days-learned % next to the existing current streak, plus a daily-average figure with its own week/month/year/all-time picker. Upcoming days now show a real forecast -- how many cards will be due, projected from today\'s actual schedule -- shaded in dashed grey to keep them visually distinct from real history. A new "All-time" view (next to "Month") lays out your whole history as a scrollable GitHub-style grid of small coloured squares, auto-scrolled to today. Tapping any day, past or future, opens a small panel with that day\'s numbers (or the forecast) and a free-text note you can attach to it -- a rough night, an exam, a reason the numbers look the way they do -- marked on the calendar with a small dot. A new gear icon lets you pick which colour drives the Volume scale (coral/green/purple/amber) and set a custom date for the streak and days-learned figures to count from, for anyone who wants those numbers to reflect a fresh start rather than this account\'s whole history.',
    'Tapping a studied day now opens that day\'s actual word list on the Browse screen (a "Showing [date]" bar, with a Clear button, filters it down) rather than just showing numbers -- every row there still has its own working flag/bury buttons. Browse also gained multi-select: a checkbox per row and a bulk-action bar for flagging or burying many words at once. "Bury for today" is new and distinct from the existing flag: a temporary, self-clearing defer -- back in rotation on its own the next study day, with no separate un-bury step -- for "not this word, right now" without committing it to the struggling-word list. Unlike flagging, burying a word frees the concurrent-learning slot it was holding, so it can actually relieve a full "Learning at once" cap rather than just hiding a word while still counting against it.',
    'Three new palette options, deliberately bolder and higher-contrast than the existing seven: Dracula and Monokai (well-known vivid-on-near-black developer themes) and Amphora, an original palette styled after black-figure and red-figure Attic pottery -- stark black paired with burnt terracotta. All three come with a full light and dark variant like every other palette.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.15.0',[
    'Fixed unnecessary GitHub API traffic that could trip the gist\'s hourly rate limit: returning to the app -- switching back from another app or tab -- while sitting on the home screen fired a fresh authenticated sync check every single time, even seconds after the last one. Ordinary alt-tabbing during a long session was enough on its own to burn through GitHub\'s 5000-requests-per-hour cap, producing a "Gist push failed (403)" toast that looked exactly like a bad or expired token but was not -- confirmed live 2026-08-15 (GitHub\'s own error was "API rate limit exceeded", not a credentials failure). That refocus check is now throttled to once a minute: returning after a real gap away still checks and catches up exactly as before, only rapid repeated refocusing is skipped. Nothing about the normal few-seconds-after-a-rating push debounce changes.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.14.5',[
    '"Learning at once" (Settings) now has an on/off switch above its two sliders, labelled "experimental" -- it\'s still being tuned against real usage data (see the "Adaptive" plan this cap came from), so there\'s now a plain way to turn it off entirely rather than only turning it down. Off reproduces exactly how new words were introduced before this cap existed on 2026.08.14.1 -- the daily "New words a day" limit is the only thing governing how many come in, with no separate concurrency ceiling at all. The two sliders stay visible only while it\'s on; switching it off hides them (they keep whatever they were set to, for whenever it\'s back on), and switching it back on picks up right where they left off, immediately, with no re-arming delay. On by default, so nothing changes for anyone not touching this setting.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.14.4',[
    'Fixed "Learning at once" (the concurrent new-word cap added 2026.08.14.1): after the very first new word, no more would come in for the rest of a sitting, no matter how high the slider was set -- effectively stuck at 1 regardless of its real number. Cause: a word held back by "Require Easy to graduate" (Settings) can sit in learning for real days, not the few minutes the cap assumed "mid-acquisition" would ever last -- if it only gets studied once a day, it can stay there indefinitely without ever graduating. Enough such words pile up over time to fill the cap by themselves and keep it full permanently, even though none of them are part of the sitting actually in front of you. The cap now only counts a word as "in flight" for the day it was first held back -- still there the next day, it\'s treated as ordinary backlog instead, and stops eating the budget meant for genuinely new words. This applies immediately to any backlog that built up before this update too, not only to words that get stuck from here on -- a word not yet reviewed today that was already stuck picks up today\'s date as if it had just been held back, rather than needing to be reviewed once more first before it starts being excluded. Nothing about "Require Easy" itself changed; a word can still take as many sittings as before to actually graduate.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.14.3',[
    'Fixed a real data-loss bug in gist sync: pairing a new device -- most likely a second "Add to Home Screen" icon, which iOS gives its own completely separate storage -- used to push that device\'s own near-empty history to the gist within seconds of typing in the gist id and token, before it had ever looked at what was already there. Reported live: a new iPhone icon silently overwriting the Mac\'s fuller review list. The cause was save()\'s automatic push, which only ever checked "is a gist id and token filled in", never "has this device actually reconciled with that gist before" -- so a device typing in credentials for the very first time was treated exactly the same as one that had been syncing for months. A device that has never reconciled with its gist now looks first instead of writing blind, the same way opening the app or returning to it already does, and only ever pushes automatically once it can prove there is nothing to lose (a genuinely empty gist, nothing pushed to it yet). Repointing an existing device at a different gist id gets the same protection, for the same reason. Nothing changes for an already-syncing device -- the normal few-seconds-after-a-rating push is untouched.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.14.2',[
    '"Learning at once" (2026.08.14.1\'s concurrent new-word cap) had its range widened from 3-6 to 3-15, and its default raised from 4 to 10 -- for anyone running a high daily new-word pace, the old ceiling of 6 sat far below their normal in-flight learning count, so with any review queue at all (essentially always) the cap held new words back completely rather than just thinning a burst, the "soft" part of the throttle never actually engaging. This does not silently reach into a device that already loaded 2026.08.14.0/.1: your settings already have a real, saved learnBufferL/E value (4, if you never opened Settings) from that first load, and a saved value always wins over the new code default -- Settings still shows exactly what it showed before this update, and only actually moving the slider changes anything. New devices, and anyone who never opened this app before today, start at 10.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.14.1',[
    'A second cap on new words, separate from the existing daily total: "Learning at once" (Settings, next to the daily allowance) limits how many brand-new words can be mid-acquisition -- not yet graduated out of learning -- in a single sitting. Split per direction, 3 to 6, default 4. This targets a different problem than the daily cap does: a burst of new words that all fail their first look together, then all come back due at the same moment a few minutes later, competing against each other with no room for any one of them to actually land -- a real pattern in his own study data, and a documented phenomenon in the memory-research and spaced-repetition literature. It\'s a soft limit -- new words only hold back while there\'s other due or already-learning work to fall back on, so a sitting never runs dry because of it; once that other work genuinely runs out, new words keep coming as before. After roughly two weeks of real use under this cap, Settings can start suggesting a personalised value for it, based on your own actual "Again" rate at each concurrency level -- nothing is ever changed for you; a suggestion only ever appears as a note you approve or dismiss.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.14.0',[
    'The automatic set-aside threshold ("Again" presses in one day before a word parks itself, so it stops appearing until you bring it back) is now a Settings slider instead of a fixed number -- 5 to 15, default 12, up from the old hardcoded 8. Raising it never brings an already-set-aside word back on its own -- it only affects what happens from here on. Lowering it applies at once to any fresh "Again" press, but never reaches backward into presses already logged earlier the same study day; those stay judged against whatever the number was when they happened, so nothing already in progress this session gets swept aside with no warning. It reaches full, unqualified effect starting your next study day.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.13.1',[
    'Backup: getting the last review or two across before switching devices should no longer take any luck. Going back to the front screen now pushes to the gist right away if a rating in the last few seconds hadn\'t been pushed yet, instead of waiting out the rest of its 6-second debounce -- pushing while the app is still fully open gives that request its best chance of actually finishing before a phone gets locked or another app opens, which is the likelier explanation for the odd review going missing than the debounce window itself. There\'s also a new "Sync now" button on the front screen, right next to the existing "backed up N ago" line, so pushing by hand no longer means a trip through Settings -- and that line now says plainly when there are reviews sitting local-only, rather than only warning once a backup has gone quiet for days.',
    'Fixed: a card could occasionally show up with no time on any of its three buttons, tappable but stuck -- leaving the queue and starting a fresh one was the only way past it. The buttons\' interval preview, and the rating itself, now recover instead of silently breaking: a preview that can\'t be computed shows "?" rather than nothing, a bad rating resets just that one card and still records the tap that triggered it, and a gap in how imported or synced settings were checked -- some scheduling-related numbers were never validated the way a card\'s own fields already were -- is closed too.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.13.0',[
    'Space/Enter, pressed a second time once the answer is showing, now rates the card Easy instead of Good -- changed from Anki\'s own convention (Space as Good) at the owner\'s request, so the fastest path through a sitting -- reveal, Space, reveal, Space -- is also the honest one, for a word that genuinely needed no thought at all. The number/letter keys (2/O for Good) and the on-screen buttons are unchanged.',
    'The study screen\'s flag -- for setting a struggling word aside by hand, mid-session -- used to appear as a small text banner ("Again N times today -- tap the flag...") once a word had drawn 5 Agains in the same study day, even though the flag itself, and the keyboard\'s F key, had actually been usable from 2 Agains onward the whole time; the banner just never said so. It\'s now a single flag icon, nothing else -- no text, no dismiss button -- sized as a proper tap target (44px), and it appears at that same lower threshold (2) the flag and the F key already used, rather than three presses late. Tapping it still sets the word aside or brings it back, with the same toast confirmation as everywhere else in the app; the F key is unchanged, still silent. The front screen\'s own "Struggling words" list, and Browse\'s per-row flag, are untouched -- both are a different, deliberately later signal (5 Agains) for what is actually worth surfacing on a curated list, not just technically flaggable.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.09.0',[
    'The study-session clock now keeps running through the "Almost there" screen (a few words still in short-term steps, back in a minute or two) instead of pausing there -- it\'s the same unbroken sitting, just waiting on the algorithm rather than off looking at Settings or Browse. The real "Nothing due" end-of-session screen still pauses it, same as leaving to any other screen. In practice this fires rarely: replaying a real backup\'s full rating history (11,305 reviews) through the actual queue-building code found only one true "nothing due" moment across the entire history, since a deck of any real size keeps the queue non-empty almost continuously -- short-term relearn steps here are 1 and 10 minutes, both comfortably inside the 20-minute "serve it a little early" window the queue already uses, so it essentially never has to make you wait. Fixed alongside it: resuming from that screen, or from a tab that got backgrounded while it was up, used to silently reset the on-screen sitting clock to zero even though the persisted daily total was unaffected -- cosmetic only, but worth closing while touching the same code.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.08.2',[
    'The daily new-word allowance ("New words a day" in Settings) is now two separate numbers instead of one shared figure -- one for each direction, so a heavier forward pace no longer silently sets the reverse direction\'s target too. The front screen\'s "+5/-5" and "ten more words" buttons now only bump whichever direction you\'re currently studying, rather than both at once. Existing settings carry forward unchanged into both directions the first time this loads -- nothing actually changes until you edit the two new fields yourself.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.08.1',[
    'The word list ("Browse the full list") can now flag or bring back any word directly, right from its row -- no need to wait until it trips the automatic Struggling-word warning, or to meet it in a session first.',
    'The practice-drill screen (for words you\'ve set aside) no longer uses multiple-choice, which turned out to be too easy to get right by elimination without actually knowing the word. Most words now get a plain typed-answer box instead -- checked leniently, so case, spacing, and accents/macrons don\'t need to be exact, since the point is whether you know the word, not whether you reproduced an accent mark. When two or more of today\'s set-aside words look alike, they\'re pulled into their own round together -- every word in that little group and its meanings appear at once, shuffled, and you match each to its meaning, so the actual distinction between them gets drilled directly rather than testing them one at a time as if they weren\'t easily confused with each other. A wrong pairing just resets rather than ending the round. A new list icon at the top of the drill screen switches to a plain, ungraded list of everything currently set aside, with its meaning right there, for reading over rather than being tested -- and switches back to practice without losing your place.',
    'Fixed: a review nudged onto your chosen weekly review day could land a day earlier than it should, if it was rated between midnight and your rollover hour -- the nudge was reading the target weekday off the exact moment you rated it, while the actual due date it was nudging landed on the study day\'s own boundary just below, and those two disagree in exactly that window.',
    'Fixed: the "on track to finish by" date on the front screen and the whole-syllabus projection could promise a finish date up to twice as soon as the honest one, for anyone studying both directions -- the pace figure feeding it counted new words met in either direction, but was being divided into a count of words remaining in only one.',
    'Fixed: a daily new-word allowance set to zero, with no study in the last two weeks, could show the words-per-day figure that projection depends on as literally "Infinity" -- which then printed as the words "Invalid Date" next to a nonsense day count, rather than a plain message that there is nothing to project yet.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.08.0',[
    'A security pass over the whole app, with no change to how anything looks or behaves. The one real hole: the Backup screen printed "Last push came from" straight into the page without escaping it, and that name is not typed on this device \u2014 it arrives from the gist or from a backup file you restore. A backup file built to abuse that could have run its own code on the page, on a page that holds your GitHub token. Every row on those panels is now escaped, centrally, so the next one added is safe without anyone having to remember.',
    'All four pages now carry a content-security policy: the app may talk to GitHub and to nothing else, and may not load a script from anywhere at all. It changes nothing you can see; it means that if something ever did get a foothold, it could not quietly send your token somewhere.',
    'A restored backup or a pulled gist is now checked harder before it is trusted: a card carrying a nonsense stability, difficulty, due date or learning step is reset to "not yet rated" rather than being fed to the scheduler, where it would have turned into a word that silently disappeared from the deck. Deliberately-crafted JSON can also no longer tamper with the shape of the state object it is loaded into.',
    'On the launcher, pairing no longer silently replaces a gist id and token that are already set up. If a scanned or pasted code points somewhere else, it now names both gists and asks first \u2014 the same way the decks already ask when two devices disagree \u2014 and Cancel really does leave everything alone.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.9',[
    'Two new keyboard shortcuts on the study screen: F flags or unflags the word on screen straight away, and X dismisses the struggling-word banner if one\'s showing, the same as tapping its own ×. F works well before the banner itself would appear -- two "Again"s today is enough, not the five a banner needs -- and does it silently, with no toast or banner of its own, since reaching for the key already means you know what you\'re doing.',
    'A new "Quick rate" setting: once a word has settled past its first learning steps, 1/I and 3/P grade it straight from the question on a keyboard, no tap to reveal first, for a fast pass through words you already know at a glance. Two flavours, since grading a word blind risks mistaking confidence for actually knowing it: "flash the answer" briefly shows it right after you grade, so a wrong guess is still visible to catch and undo; "fully blind" skips that. Off by default, and new or still-learning words always go through the ordinary reveal-then-rate regardless of the setting -- that\'s the one place getting it wrong quietly costs the most.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.8',[
    'A rating made right before leaving -- closing the tab, switching apps, locking the screen -- inside the 6-second window save() waits before pushing to the gist could be dropped silently: the debounced push was purely time-based, with nothing forcing it out early if the tab went away first. No error, no toast, the gist just quietly sat a review or two behind until the next rating happened to trigger a fresh push. The tab going hidden (backgrounding, switching apps, locking the screen) or actually closing/reloading now forces that pending push out immediately instead of waiting on the timer, using the same two hooks (visibilitychange, pagehide) that already paused the study-time clock for the same moments. Nothing changes for a normal sitting where more ratings keep arriving inside the 6s window -- the debounce still coalesces those into one push, same as before.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.7',[
    'A one-day review rated in the evening or at night could come back a day and a half, or even two days, later instead of the next day. 2026.08.07.3\'s fix for words repeating inside the same sitting rounded a card up to the boundary after next whenever the immediate one was closer than half the interval just earned -- twelve hours, for a one-day card -- and since every rollover option here sits in the small hours (midnight, 3am, 4am, 6am), an ordinary evening or night session almost always fell in that back half and got bumped a full extra day for nothing. Two changes: the round-up now only fires within three hours of the rollover, not twelve; and the actual guarantee against a same-sitting repeat moved to where it belongs, the study queue\'s own learn-ahead check, which now only treats a card as a genuine short step if it was scheduled under an hour out -- well above the ten minutes a real short step ever runs, and well below anything the day-snap can still produce, so the two can\'t be confused regardless of how the three-hour figure above is ever retuned again. Re-checked the original incident directly: a card rated 03:45 against a 4am rollover still lands cleanly a day out and is never handed back inside the same sitting; a card rated at 9pm now lands seven hours out, on tomorrow\'s boundary, instead of being pushed to the day after.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.6',[
    'The Stats screen gained a new "Time & learning cost" section: real time studied today/this week/this month, seconds spent per review broken down by what stage the word was at (new, in short-term steps, settling, holding), a real measured split between time spent on new words versus reviews (last 14 days), a fastest/slowest-to-learn word list, and a histogram of how long words actually take to learn -- with toggles for active thinking time vs. calendar time since first meeting the word, and for grouping by stage or by part. The review/new split needed data the app wasn\'t collecting before now: the study-screen stopwatch (studyMs, 2026.08.07.4) only ever credited a whole sitting\'s segment at once, with no record of which card\'s stage that time belonged to. Ratings now also credit the gap since the previous one -- exactly the time spent looking at the card just rated -- to that card\'s own stage, summed into a new per-day stageMs/stageN pair alongside the existing total. That\'s real, forward-only data: days before this build show no split, rather than a guessed one -- filling those in with a modelled estimate is a separate, not-yet-built piece. The fastest/slowest list and histogram reuse the Browse screen\'s existing per-word "time to learn" figure (2026.08.05.1), generalised to one pass over the whole log instead of one word at a time, and only count a word once it has at least 3 timed readings, so one lucky or unlucky reading can\'t swing either.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.5',[
    'The launcher page (the one linking to Latin, Λέξεις, the manual, and Backup & sync) now shows the current version too, linked straight through to this list -- before this, seeing what changed meant opening a deck first and tapping the build line at the bottom of its own front screen. Reaching it from the launcher needed one small addition here: opening either deck at a #changelog link now shows this screen right away on load, instead of always landing on the front screen first and requiring a second tap.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.4',[
    'Two new figures, both requested: a running clock in the study screen, under the new/learning/due counts, showing how long today\'s sitting has actually taken; and a "rated today · studied" line on the front screen giving the same total for the whole day, every sitting summed. Deliberately real wall-clock time rather than a derived estimate -- the only per-review timing that existed before this was thinkMs (seeing the word to revealing the answer), which never covered the pause between reveal and actually choosing Again/Good/Easy, so any estimate built from it alone was undercounting real time spent by a real margin. The study-screen clock resets to zero each time you walk in fresh, keeps running through a brief backgrounding within the same sitting (a call, a notification), and pauses -- crediting whatever ran -- the instant the tab is hidden or you leave for anywhere else in the app, so time spent elsewhere is never counted as study time. Persisted per study day (S.days[k].studyMs) and split across the rollover boundary if a sitting straddles it, so a session starting before the day rolls over and finishing after credits each day correctly instead of dumping the whole segment on whichever one the flush happened to land on.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.3',[
    'Words could come back for a second go inside the same sitting, and the "learning" count could climb by one with every single word rated, when studying in the run-up to the daily reset. A review card\'s next date is deliberately rounded down onto a day boundary, so that everything scheduled for a day unlocks together at the rollover rather than at whatever o\'clock it happened to be rated -- but the rounding had no floor, so a word earning a one-day gap at 03:45 against a 4am reset was rounded down to 04:00 and came back fifteen minutes later, or one minute later if rated at 03:59. The 20-minute learn-ahead window then handed it straight back. Measured before the fix: a 40-card queue started at 03:42 needed 80 ratings to clear, every card twice. The rounding now steps up to the following boundary whenever rounding down would swallow more than half the gap that was actually earned -- which can only ever affect a one-day gap, and only in the back half of a study day.',
    'In the last 20 minutes before the daily reset, the whole of the next day\'s review load was being counted as "learning" and served early -- and each word served that way was then rescheduled from the old day, walking its interval a day earlier every time a late-night session happened. The learn-ahead window now only reaches words genuinely sitting on a minutes-long step, not ones simply scheduled for the next day.',
    'Changing "A study day begins at" still moved every review card\'s date, one load later than before. 2026.08.07.0 stopped the setting taking effect retroactively, but a separate snap in the load path -- described in the code as a one-time migration, in fact re-running on every load, every gist pull and every file restore, always under whatever hour was current -- re-bucketed every review card as soon as the deferred change committed and was saved: measured at 22 hours earlier per card for a 4am to 6am move. That snap has been removed; it had had nothing left to migrate for over a week, and it was also quietly wrecking any word being held on a 10-minute drill step.',
    'Bringing a word back after it was set aside automatically did not survive a reload. The check that sets a word aside after eight "Again" presses in one day reads the review log, which never forgets, so it re-flagged the word on the next load however deliberately it had been brought back -- which meant the flag, the struggling list, the drill\'s "bring back" and Settings\' "bring back everything" were all impossible to make stick for the rest of that day, on exactly the words a bad sitting had just flagged. A bring-back now holds for the rest of that study day; the next day\'s eight Agains flag the word again as before.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.2',[
    '.1 (below), shipped minutes earlier the same day, fixed the truncated-gist-content problem but introduced a second, narrower bug in the same function: the fallback fetch to raw_url carried an Authorization header "to be safe", which doesn\'t need one (a secret gist\'s raw content is unlisted, not access-controlled) and turned it into a non-simple cross-origin request -- which the raw-content host doesn\'t answer a CORS preflight for, so the browser blocked the request before it went out, surfacing as an opaque "Load failed"/"Failed to fetch" with no HTTP status attached. Caught because that specific failure mode -- a real cross-origin request actually being sent and rejected by the browser itself -- can only show up against the real endpoints; a same-session test using a stand-in fetch() had verified the fallback logic\'s branching correctly but, standing in for the network entirely, could never have exercised CORS either way. Fixed by dropping that header; confirmed this time with real, unmocked requests against the live gist.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.1',[
    'Gist pull -- both the silent one on launch and Settings\' "Pull from gist now" -- could fail with no visible sign anything was wrong, once a save grew large enough. GitHub\'s gist API truncates a file\'s content in the response past a size it doesn\'t document precisely for a multi-file gist (a combined budget across this gist\'s files, in practice, well under the ~1MB nominally quoted per file); a large save came back cut off mid-object, failed to parse, and that parse failure was swallowed by the same catch used for a genuine network error -- so a big enough backup just quietly stopped restoring on other devices, with no toast, while push (which sends its own request body rather than reading this field back) kept working, and the gist itself never fell behind. Fixed by checking the API\'s own truncated flag on the file -- and, since it was observed not lining up exactly with what actually got cut, falling back on any parse failure regardless of that flag -- and re-fetching the file\'s raw_url, which returns it whole up to 10MB. Verified against a real backup already past the failure size: the old code silently returned nothing, the fixed code retrieves and parses the full file.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.07.0',[
    'Changing "A study day begins at" (Settings) used to take effect immediately -- including retroactively, for cards already scheduled under the old hour. Moving the hour later in the day (say 4am to 6am) could, on the very next load, throw a chunk of tomorrow\'s (or even later today\'s) reviews straight into "due right now", since the day-boundary math had no way left to tell "today" from "yesterday" once the rule under it changed mid-day. The setting now takes effect from the next time the old hour would have rolled the day over anyway, not before -- nothing already scheduled is ever reinterpreted, so picking a new hour can never make a card look due that it wasn\'t a moment before. Settings shows when a pending change actually kicks in, and the schedule keeps running on the old hour until then.',
    'The same-day Good/Hard drill loop (Settings\' "Only Easy clears it") used to clear a word after exactly 3 same-day tries without an Easy, hardcoded. A new slider next to it, 3 through 10, makes that count adjustable -- lower drills a stubborn word less before letting it through, higher drills it harder. Leaving it at the default of 3 behaves exactly as before.',
    'An automatic sync (another device\'s copy taken on launch, no prompt shown) now says so if it changed how many reviews are waiting -- "due went 12 → 91" alongside the usual "Synced -- N reviews from [device]" -- instead of leaving a changed count to look unexplained.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.06.0',[
    'A setting synced in from another device, pulled from a gist backup, or restored from a saved file could look right without acting right: Settings would correctly show it switched on, but nothing on screen changed to match -- keyboard hints on the study buttons, review history on the card, even light/dark theme and the colour palette, all stayed stuck at whatever this device had shown before, until something else happened to force a redraw. Toggling that very same setting off and back on was the reliable way to "fix" it, since that\'s the one place that redraw already ran from -- which is what made it look like the setting itself was misremembered, when it never actually was. All four of those paths (a launch sync pulling a newer copy, resolving a sync conflict, restoring a backup file, and a manual gist pull) share one function that swaps in the incoming state; it just never told the screen to redraw once the swap was done. Fixed there once, for all four at once, rather than at each call site.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.05.1',[
    'The word list (Browse) now answers "how long has this actually taken me" for any word you\'ve met at least once: tap to reveal it, same as always, and underneath the answer sits how many reviews it\'s had and how long ago the first one was, plus a rough estimate of the active thinking time behind them -- summed from how long you paused before revealing the answer on each of those reviews. A reading is left out of that sum (and the line says how many were) if it looked unreliable at the time -- you\'d looked away, or paused suspiciously long -- the same guard reveal() already applies before a reading is even allowed to nudge a grade, tightened further here since summing dozens of readings is far less forgiving of an occasional bad one than adjusting a single grade is. Nothing is computed for a word until you actually tap it open, so a list of several thousand words costs nothing extra just for existing. Requested days ago and deliberately parked until now -- see the design note in HANDOFF.md for why it took two attempts to get the "already touched today" logic right once .05.0\'s drill-loop cap entered the picture.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.05.0',[
    'A learning or relearning word held under Settings\' "Require Easy to graduate" could loop forever if it only ever earned Good or Hard, never Easy -- the same-day 10-minute hold had no exit besides an eventual Easy press. It now clears itself after DRILL_CAP (3) same-day tries without one, graduating at whatever stability that final rating actually earned (never Easy\'s, since Easy was never pressed) rather than circling the same 10-minute interval indefinitely. Tracked on the card\'s own loops/loopDay pair rather than streak/lastDay, since those are deliberately reverted for the duration of the hold and would reset to zero on every single loop instead of remembering the count. Hard counts toward the cap the same as Good; a card that relapses (an Again after graduating) needs a fresh run of DRILL_CAP loops afterward, same as a first attempt. The unrelated drillDays (mature-review, consecutive-day) hold is untouched.',
    'The full calendar\'s per-day box (2026.08.04.2) showed two numbers -- new words, and "reviewed" (every rating that wasn\'t a word\'s first-ever rep). Replaced with three clearer ones: new words learnt, distinct words touched (new or repeat -- a leech failed twice today, or a new word stepping through two learning steps in one sitting, now counts once here instead of twice), and the raw number of ratings made. The distinct-word count needed its own new per-day counter rather than being derivable after the fact, and needed care to get right: the obvious signal (a card\'s lastDay) turned out to be unsafe combined with this same build\'s DRILL_CAP hold, which deliberately reverts lastDay on every same-day loop -- caught by an end-to-end test before it shipped, not by inspection. A day recorded before this field existed shows a dash for the middle number rather than a false zero.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.11',[
    'The two columns in the wide Home/Settings layout could land badly uneven -- one holding three cards, the other four, sometimes 300px+ taller -- because CSS column-count only ever fills column 1 first and overflows into column 2 in strict document order; with only a handful of large, break-inside:avoid cards, that heuristic has no way to correct for one oversized card (the whole-syllabus stats block) landing alone in a column. .9\'s "explicit nudge" fixed a different failure mode entirely (both columns collapsing into one on some early layout passes) and left this one untouched. column-count is gone now, replaced on both .homeCols and .setCols by a small balanceColumns() function: it measures each card at its real column width, then places each one whole -- never split -- into whichever of two explicit column divs is currently shorter, which a browser\'s own sequential-fill heuristic can\'t do from three or four large chunks. Testing this live turned up a second, unrelated bug in the process: every "back to home"/"into settings" handler in this file calls its render function before the screen is actually shown, so a hidden (display:none) screen was measuring 0 for every card and piling all of them into one column regardless of balanceColumns() itself being correct -- fixed by also re-balancing once the screen is actually visible, so it no longer depends on getting call order right at every individual site. Verified directly in a real (Chromium) browser: repeated portrait/landscape cycling, opening and closing a details fold mid-session, and every Study→Home/Settings→Home navigation path all leave both columns within a few percent of each other, with no card ever split.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.10',[
    'Vocabula now ships its own icon for Add to Home Screen / Add to Dock -- a capital V in the same Palatino the wordmark already uses, on the vellum theme\'s own paper colour, with a small corner badge carrying the current build\'s last digit. iOS and macOS snapshot an icon the moment it\'s added and there is no way for an installed web app to update its own icon afterward -- a fresh badge only shows up after removing and re-adding it, the same "has to actually relaunch" limitation the app itself already has. The badge wraps 0-9 rather than growing a second digit on a day with more than ten builds, since a single glyph reads far better at icon size than the full version string would.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.9',[
    'Two reported glitches on first launch, both traced to the same neighbourhood: the wide layout could show as the narrow one until something else forced a redraw, and Home or Settings could render as a single column until a details section was opened. Root cause for the first: the check that decides wide-vs-narrow only ever ran once at boot, reading the screen size before a freshly-launched "Add to Dock" window had necessarily finished settling into its real dimensions -- with no later correction if that one read landed wrong. A second check now runs shortly after boot, and the screen size is also rechecked on every window resize, not only on the specific transition a browser chooses to report. Root cause for the second, most likely: Home and Settings both hold their content in a two-column box that can balance itself into one column on an early layout pass and never redo the sum on its own -- opening a details section happens to force that redo as a side effect, which is what was actually being seen, not something the details section itself does. Both screens now get an explicit nudge shortly after boot, and again whenever the layout genuinely switches into the wide version, so the redo no longer depends on happening to open something.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.8',[
    'A small recap card can now sit in the top-right corner of the wide study layout, showing the word you just rated, what you pressed, and how far it jumped forward -- there for anyone who was reaching for undo just to double-check. Shares the review-history switch in Settings rather than adding a fourth one.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.7',[
    'The "New"/"Legacy" layout choice is gone from Settings -- the wider two-pane study screen (and the matching layouts for the front screen, Settings and Browse) now switches on automatically on any screen at least 700px wide in landscape, for everyone, the same way it would have under "New". A phone held upright is unaffected either way. In its place: three independent switches under "Study screen, wide layout" -- review history on the card (times seen, streak, last result), keyboard hints written on the buttons, and a small amount of animation on reveal and between cards -- each off by default, each only doing anything in that same wide layout. Animation always defers to your system\'s reduced-motion setting regardless of this switch.',
    'Undo now also answers to the <b>0</b> key, alongside the existing <b>Z</b> -- both sit under the same right hand as the 1/2/3 rating alternative (I/O/P), so a hand already resting there for rating never has to move for either one.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.6',[
    'The grading-rule reminder (Settings and the manual) used to count a multi-meaning word as known once you\'d produced the first sense plus one other. Changed to require every sense listed -- a word you can only partly recall isn\'t one you know yet, and the schedule shouldn\'t treat it as one. This is guidance only: the app can\'t check what you actually recalled, so nothing in the code changes -- it takes effect the next time you press a button on a multi-meaning word.',
    'Review cards (words due for a normal review, not new or relearning) are now served in roughly-shuffled order instead of strictly oldest-due-first: cards close in overdue-ness are shuffled against each other freely, but a card that has been due for weeks still tends to come well before one just due today. Priority between buckets is unchanged -- a lapsed word still comes back ahead of everything else once its short relearn timer is up, and unseen words still introduce in the same fixed order as always. Only the ordering within the review queue itself changed.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.5',[
    'The "New" landscape layout now covers the Drill screen too -- it never got this treatment when the layout first shipped, since Drill was built afterward. Same two-pane shape as Study: the word fills its own full-height card on the left, the multiple-choice answers, "bring this word back" link and score sit grouped and centred on the right. Nothing changes on a phone, or with Layout left on "Legacy".'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.4',[
    'The shared gist credentials added in .3 could, in one specific case, have quietly switched which gist a device backs up to: if the two decks had ever been set up with genuinely different gist ids or tokens, whichever one loaded first after that update would have won, silently, from then on. Fixed before it could actually happen to anyone -- a real disagreement between this device\'s own working setting and the shared one now stops and asks (naming both, never printing the token) instead of picking one automatically. Nothing changes for the normal case, one gist already serving both decks.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.3',[
    'Device name, gist id and gist token can now be set once from the launcher page (the "Vocabula" link screen in front of both decks) instead of typed into each deck\'s own Settings separately -- useful if you open both from the same pinned launcher rather than pinning Latin and Greek as two separate Home Screen icons. Set from either place; both decks pick it up the next time they load. Purely additive: a deck pinned on its own, the original setup this app has always documented, behaves exactly as before -- nothing to share, nothing changes. The token still never leaves the device or gets written into the gist or a backup file.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.2',[
    'The full calendar (front screen -> Full calendar) now shows every studied day\'s new-word and review counts directly on its box -- new in green, reviewed in amber -- so a whole month is readable at a glance instead of needing a tap per day. Tapping a day for its own detail line, and the longest-streak note underneath the grid, are both gone now that the same numbers already sit right there on every box; nothing else about the calendar changed.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.1',[
    'Finale -- a stability pass, not a new feature push: a full read-through of the whole codebase looking specifically for the kind of thing that causes another fix to be needed the next day rather than a wrong number today. Found and fixed one small, real inconsistency: the daily theme rotation and a successful gist push each updated this browser\'s storage but not its second mirror, so the two could briefly disagree before the next rating re-synced them. Both are now written together every time, like every save already does.',
    'The struggling-word warning on the study card now has a small X below the flag: it dismisses that one warning without setting the word aside, so acknowledging a rough day for a word and actually flagging it are no longer the same tap. The automatic set-aside at 8 "Again"s today is unchanged -- it still happens on its own regardless of whether the warning was dismissed.',
    'A "Drill these words" mode for everything you\'ve set aside, reachable from the front screen\'s Set aside list: a multiple-choice pass, reshuffled fresh every time you start it and with fresh answer choices on every single card, specifically so the order stops being something you can learn instead of the word. Two words that just look alike (close spelling, not merely both being hard) are put next to each other in the drill and offered as each other\'s wrong answer first, so the pair actually gets told apart instead of drifting further apart in memory. None of this touches real scheduling, so it can be run as many times a day as wanted.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.04.0',[
    'The next word\'s answer flashing on screen, again -- this time actually caught frame by frame in a screen recording (not inferred): every single rating, in the Safari "Add to Dock" app, the *new* card\'s answer was momentarily visible before fading down to hidden, the opposite of the fade *in* it\'s meant to do when you tap "Show answer" yourself. The last two attempts (2026.08.03.3 and .5) both tried to time a CSS fade-out animation out of the way for the split second the next card appears; both held up in testing here and both still failed in real Safari. Rather than time it more carefully a third time, the fade-out is gone -- there is nothing left for a badly-timed repaint to animate. The reveal fade-in you do want is unaffected and still plays. Not yet re-confirmed against a fresh recording in real Safari, since nothing here can run it directly -- flag it again if it recurs.',
    'The "New" landscape layout\'s study card no longer reads as empty: the word now fills its own full-height card instead of floating alone in a mostly blank pane, the answer and rating buttons are grouped and centred as a block instead of pinned to the bottom of a mostly empty column, and both get noticeably bigger text that actually grows with a wider window rather than stopping at a phone-sized maximum. Nothing changes for Home, Settings or Browse, and nothing changes at all unless Layout is switched to "New".'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.03.6',[
    'The "New" landscape layout (Settings > Layout) was, fairly, called out as looking quite broken -- and it was. Three real bugs, all only visible with that setting switched on: (1) the study screen\'s two-pane grid never actually turned itself off when you left the study screen, so its "Show answer" button could sit stranded over the front screen or Settings; (2) the front screen\'s two-column layout could silently spill into a 3rd or 4th column off the right edge of the screen, taking most of its cards with it -- invisible, with no way to scroll to them; (3) Settings had the exact same silent-overflow problem. All three fixed. Nothing changes for anyone who has not switched Layout to "New".'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.03.5',[
    'The 2026.08.03.3 fix for the next word\'s answer flashing on screen did not actually hold up -- a screen recording caught it still happening, on every single rating, specifically in the Safari "Add to Dock" app (not reproducible in a Chromium-based browser, which is very likely why .3 looked confirmed when it wasn\'t). The previous attempt suppressed the fade animation, swapped in the new answer, forced the browser to redraw immediately, then turned the animation back on -- correct in principle, but forcing a redraw only guarantees the layout has updated, not that the fade-off state has actually been painted, which is evidently not the same thing in Safari. It now waits for two real screen redraws, not just a forced recalculation, before turning the fade animation back on. Not verified live in Safari itself this time either -- there was no way to test that directly -- so this is the best diagnosis available from a recording plus a browser that can\'t reproduce the Safari-specific half of it; worth a fresh recording before calling it fully closed.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.03.4',[
    'On a keyboard, rating a card with 1/2/3 always reached across the number row with your left hand. I/O/P -- the row directly below 1/2/3 on the right hand -- now do exactly the same thing, so either hand works. Both sets of keys are live at once; nothing about 1/2/3 changed.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.03.3',[
    'Fixed, for real this time: the next word\'s answer could flash on screen for an instant right after you rated a card, before you had tapped to reveal it. The 2026.08.02.0 fix guessed this was a fast-double-tap timing race and added a safeguard against that -- a real fix for a real (rarer) issue, kept as-is -- but it turns out the actual cause was simpler and happened on every single card, not just fast taps: showing the next word\'s answer text and hiding it happen in the same instant, but the fade animation did not know that, so it played its hide-animation over the word that had just been swapped in rather than over nothing. Confirmed directly this time (not just reported) before fixing it.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.03.2',[
    'New Settings > Layout option, "Legacy" or "New" -- Legacy is what you already have, and stays the default; nothing changes unless you switch it. "New" only changes anything on a screen at least 700px wide, turned sideways: a laptop or an iPad. There, the study card becomes a two-pane spread instead of one stretched column, the front screen\'s sections group into two columns instead of one long scroll, Settings reads in two columns, and the word-list browser gets a fixed search-and-filter panel beside the results instead of one that scrolls away above a long list. On a phone in your hand the two look identical either way -- there is no "always sideways" option, since that would read wrong held upright.',
    'The study card and its answer no longer jump to a bigger size at exactly 700px wide -- they now scale continuously with the CSS clamp() function, reaching the exact same maximum size they always did by 700px, just without the single jump.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.03.1',[
    '"Erase all progress" now pushes a backup to the gist and waits for that push to actually succeed before wiping anything, instead of trying a file download and erasing 700ms later regardless of whether it worked. Needs gist backup set up in Settings -- without it, erasing now refuses rather than fall back to a download that Safari can silently swallow.',
    'Two spots that separately computed your recent daily pace now share one calculation. No visible change.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.03.0',[
    'Changed how the struggling-word flag counts, at the owner\'s request: it now counts raw "Again" presses within a single study day, not distinct study days as it briefly did. Getting a word wrong 5 times today shows the flag on the card and in the front screen\'s Struggling words list; 8 times today sets it aside on its own. A word set aside stays aside regardless of the day, exactly as before -- only the trigger changed.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.02.0',[
    'Fixed (reported, not yet reproduced live -- read Settings > What changed for the mechanism if it recurs): a fast double-tap could very rarely flash the next word\'s answer for an instant. Rating a word swaps in the next one and resets its "show answer" button to the exact same spot the rating button had just been -- if the second half of a fast double-tap landed there, it revealed the new word immediately. The tap that reveals a card now waits a fraction of a second after that card first appears, the same way rating already waited a fraction of a second after a reveal.',
    'The "Study days" strip now expands into a real calendar: tap "Full calendar" for a month at a time, with a dot for each day you studied, tap-a-day for how many new words and how many reviews, and your longest streak alongside the current one.',
    'A new "Browse the full list" screen from the front screen: every word in the deck, either direction, grouped by part and alphabetised, with a search box, a filter by how well you know each one, and a tap to reveal the answer without it counting as a review.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.01.2',[
    'A maintenance pass, requested and reviewed rather than routine: a startup call was re-rendering the front screen\'s warning banner a second time for no reason, the front screen was rebuilding the study queue three times over when two would do, and six separate "back to the front screen" buttons were wired with six copies of the same two lines (one of them in a different order, for no reason). None of these changed anything you could see; all were just doing the same work more than once.',
    'The background timer that resumes a card after "Almost there" now skips its tick while the tab is out of view, instead of quietly doing the same pointless check every five seconds whether or not the phone is even awake to show it.',
    'Every single rating was rebuilding the front screen\'s warning banner. Studying Greek to English that cost nothing worth measuring, but in the English-to-Greek direction with the unlock gate on it meant rebuilding the whole reverse queue and re-checking every word\'s gate -- to update a banner sitting on a screen you are not looking at while studying. It now only does that work when the front screen is actually up.',
    'The flag added last time now looks at just the one word in front of you instead of rebuilding a tally of every word that has ever earned an Again, every time a card is shown or rated -- same numbers, less work each time, which only matters more as the review log keeps growing.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.01.1',[
    'Fixed: the "eight failures sets a word aside" mechanic never actually worked — a lapse counter was tracked and a tag appeared on the card, but nothing ever set a word aside, and there was no way to do it by hand either. Words already past eight are set aside retroactively on this update.',
    'A flag on the study card once "Again" has landed on a word on 5 separate days (not 5 presses — several in one sitting only count once): tap it to set the word aside by hand. From 8 separate days it happens on its own.',
    'The front screen gained a "Struggling words" section: every word past 5 separate Again-days, ranked worst first, and the words you have actually set aside, both with a flag to toggle right from the list.'
  ]],
  ['2026.08.01.0',[
    'Space now doubles as Good once the answer is showing, not just as reveal — matching the same Space key convention Anki uses, so the single most common rating no longer needs a second key.',
    'A name for this device (Settings, above the gist fields): stamped onto anything pushed to the gist or saved as a backup file. When this device and the gist have both moved on since the last sync, you are now asked which copy to keep, and both are named ("This device (iPhone) has 2 reviews the gist does not have... last pushed by Mac...") instead of a generic prompt pointing at Settings. The front screen, Settings and the "what happens on its own" panel all show which device a backup came from.'
  ]],
  ['2026.07.31.2',[
    'A stats page, next to the settings icon on the front screen: a bar showing where every word in the whole list currently stands (still to meet, in short-term steps, settling, holding), and a grid of little boxes per part that fills in as words move through those stages.'
  ]],
  ['2026.07.31.1',[
    'Fixed: the wider layout meant to kick in on iPad, Mac and phones turned sideways (a centred column, bigger type) had stopped taking effect — a later, unconditional style rule was silently cancelling it. Rotating to landscape now actually scales the card again.'
  ]],
  ['2026.07.31.0',[
    'Catppuccin, Rose Pine, Kanagawa, Gruvbox and Everforest are more saturated -- richer, less washed-out, especially the rating buttons. Everforest\'s Good button in dark mode was particularly pastel and is now an actual forest green. Vellum and Legacy are unchanged. Checked against the original contrast on every button; none got harder to read, several improved.'
  ]],
  ['2026.07.30.3',[
    'Fixed: sitting on \'Almost there\' past the rollover hour could silently drop you into a new study day\'s full word batch, with no tap on Start. The five-second check that resumes a near-term learning card no longer reaches for a fresh day\'s words -- it now waits for the Done screen to catch up instead.'
  ]],
  ['2026.07.30.2',[
    'With the study day set to begin at midnight or 3am, every launch pulled review dates onto the wrong day — up to a day early. Loading now respects the hour you chose.',
    'The intervals shown on the buttons for a word carried over from the audit now match what pressing them does. The first look at such a word could promise weeks or years where the true result was a day.',
    'The fortnight forecast counts by study day, so a card due at tomorrow\'s rollover no longer shows under Today.',
    'Settings now reports the day-start hour you chose instead of always saying 4am.',
    'The manual and a leftover hidden setting no longer describe the automatic fallback download that the previous build removed.',
    'The manual now says how audit carryovers actually arrive: through the daily allowance, ahead of brand-new words, with their audit strength as a head start.'
  ]],
  ['2026.07.30.1',[
    'A review card held on a short step by the drill setting is now served by the learn-ahead too. Previously the day could end with those cards pending and nothing on screen.',
    'Reviews are now due by day rather than by clock time: everything scheduled for a day unlocks together at the rollover, so an afternoon batch is no longer held back until the afternoon.',
    'A study day can begin at midnight, 3am, 4am or 6am.',
    'Elapsed time is counted in study days. A review at 22:30 and another at 05:30 used to read as zero days apart, and was scheduled as a same-day repetition.',
    'The daily new-word allowance is counted separately per direction. Forward words used to consume the reverse direction\'s allowance too.',
    'No more automatic file downloads \u2014 Safari blocked them silently, so they never worked. The gist is the automatic backup; files are saved by hand.',
    'Undo no longer survives a restore, where it could truncate the restored log.',
    'Undo across a rollover no longer writes yesterday\'s counts into today.',
    'Relearning cards count toward the recall rate.',
    'One gist push per save instead of two.',
    'Set-aside words no longer distort the syllabus estimate.'
  ]],
  ['2026.07.31',[
    'Seven themes, each with its own light and dark variant. Vellum is now the default; Legacy is the original palette.',
    'A theme can rotate daily, in a shuffled cycle that shows every one before repeating any.',
    'The gist is the primary backup. The file download is now only a fallback for when the gist is off or failing.',
    'The gist token is no longer written into the gist or into backup files. It was, and GitHub kept revoking it.',
    'Learning cards are served up to 20 minutes early when nothing else is left, so the day no longer ends with cards stranded mid-step.',
    'Changing the day\'s new-word allowance survives an undo. It used to be silently reverted.',
    'A syllabus panel on the front screen: expected recall now, and on term day.',
    'This screen, and a panel in settings listing every background process.'
  ]],
  ['Earlier',[
    'FSRS scheduling seeded from the audit, part chips, timing-based grade adjustment, the reverse-direction gate, and the pace panel.'
  ]]
];
